-- Physics: syllabus gap-fill MCQs PHY-7001..7028 and a difficult set PHY-7101..7300 (228 questions).
-- Gap audit: every PMDC MDCAT 2025 and NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics objective was matched against the live bank again. The
-- thin ones get new questions here: biological effects and medical uses of radiation (NUMS 13.4, had none), Newton's
-- second law as rate of change of momentum, electric power and kWh, launch angles with equal range, projectiles
-- launched from a height, speeds of approach and separation, and potential energy below the reference level.
-- Difficult set: 200 multi-step numericals and conceptual traps across all 14 MDCAT Physics chapters, every answer
-- recomputed when written. Original questions in the bank's template. Own id block (7001 up), counters untouched.
-- questions.chapter is the MDCAT chapter. NUMS rows only where the NUMS 2026 syllabus names the topic; every question
-- is in the AKU bank; FSc rows only where the PECTAA textbook chapter teaches the topic. Not in the free set.

-- Inserted in batches of 20 to stay under D1's statement size limit.

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-7001', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Biological effects of radiation', 'easy', 'NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics 13.4 (biological effects and medical uses of radiation); not on the PMDC MDCAT 2025 syllabus', 'Ionizing radiation harms living tissue mainly because it', '["ionizes atoms and molecules in cells, breaking chemical bonds and damaging DNA", "raises the temperature of the tissue by many degrees", "makes the exposed tissue itself radioactive", "changes the mass of the cells it passes through"]', 0, 'Ionizing radiation knocks electrons out of atoms. The ions and free radicals it creates break bonds in molecules such as DNA, which can kill cells or make them grow abnormally. The heating it causes is tiny.', '{"1": "The energy deposited by a harmful dose would warm tissue by only a tiny fraction of a degree.", "2": "Alpha, beta and gamma radiation do not make the body radioactive; only neutron exposure can do that, and it is not the main harm.", "3": "Radiation does not measurably change the mass of cells."}'),
  ('PHY-7002', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Medical uses of radiation', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and particle physics (§19.6 Radioactive tracers, medical use); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics 13.4', 'Radiotherapy can destroy a tumour while sparing most of the surrounding tissue because cancer cells', '["do not absorb any gamma radiation", "divide rapidly, so they absorb more radiation and are more easily destroyed by it", "contain no DNA that radiation can damage", "are always located near the skin surface"]', 1, 'Rapidly dividing cells are the most sensitive to ionizing radiation, so a carefully aimed and controlled dose kills tumour cells more readily than normal cells. Some healthy tissue is still damaged, which is why patients often feel ill.', '{"0": "If tumour cells absorbed no radiation, radiotherapy would have no effect on them.", "2": "All living cells, including cancer cells, contain DNA.", "3": "Tumours can be deep inside the body; gamma beams and implanted seeds reach them."}'),
  ('PHY-7003', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Biological effects of radiation', 'moderate', 'NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics 13.4 (biological effects and medical uses of radiation); not on the PMDC MDCAT 2025 syllabus', 'The SI unit of absorbed dose, the radiation energy absorbed per kilogram of tissue, is the', '["sievert (Sv)", "becquerel (Bq)", "gray (Gy)", "curie (Ci)"]', 2, 'Absorbed dose = energy absorbed ÷ mass, measured in J kg⁻¹, which is called the gray (1 Gy = 1 J kg⁻¹).', '{"0": "The sievert measures equivalent (effective) dose, which also weighs how harmful each type of radiation is.", "1": "The becquerel measures the activity of a source (decays per second).", "3": "The curie is an old unit of activity, not of dose."}'),
  ('PHY-7004', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Biological effects of radiation', 'moderate', 'NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics 13.4 (biological effects and medical uses of radiation); not on the PMDC MDCAT 2025 syllabus', 'The sievert differs from the gray because the sievert', '["measures the number of decays per second of a source", "is used only for gamma rays", "measures the energy of a single photon", "takes into account how damaging the particular type of radiation is to living tissue"]', 3, 'Equivalent dose (Sv) = absorbed dose (Gy) × a radiation weighting factor. For the same energy absorbed, alpha particles do about 20 times as much biological damage as gamma rays, so the same number of grays gives more sieverts for alpha.', '{"0": "Decays per second is activity, measured in becquerels.", "1": "The sievert is used for all ionizing radiations, not just gamma.", "2": "Photon energy is measured in joules or electronvolts."}'),
  ('PHY-7005', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Biological effects of radiation', 'moderate', 'NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics 13.4 (biological effects and medical uses of radiation); not on the PMDC MDCAT 2025 syllabus', 'Radiation damage to the DNA of reproductive cells, which may be passed on to a person''s children, is called a', '["genetic (hereditary) effect", "somatic effect", "thermal effect", "photoelectric effect"]', 0, 'Genetic effects arise from mutations in sperm or egg cells and can appear in later generations. Somatic effects (burns, cataracts, cancer) appear in the exposed person.', '{"1": "Somatic effects appear in the body of the exposed person, not in the offspring.", "2": "Radiation doses that cause harm produce negligible heating.", "3": "The photoelectric effect is the emission of electrons from a metal by light."}'),
  ('PHY-7006', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Biological effects of radiation', 'hard', 'NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics 13.4 (biological effects and medical uses of radiation); not on the PMDC MDCAT 2025 syllabus', 'An alpha source is fairly safe to hold briefly outside the body but very dangerous if swallowed or inhaled because alpha particles', '["turn into gamma rays once they are inside the body", "are stopped by the dead outer layer of skin, but inside the body they deposit all their energy in a tiny volume of living cells", "travel much farther through tissue than gamma rays do", "are attracted to the bones by their positive charge"]', 1, 'Alpha particles are the most strongly ionizing and least penetrating radiation. Outside the body the skin''s dead layer stops them; inside, every bit of their energy goes into nearby living cells, causing intense local damage.', '{"0": "Alpha particles do not change into gamma rays.", "2": "Alpha particles have the shortest range of the three radiations (a few cm of air, a fraction of a mm of tissue).", "3": "Charge plays no role in where a swallowed isotope collects; that depends on its chemistry."}'),
  ('PHY-7007', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Radiation safety', 'hard', 'NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics 13.4 (biological effects and medical uses of radiation); not on the PMDC MDCAT 2025 syllabus', 'A worker stands 1 m from a small gamma source. Ignoring absorption in air, if the worker moves to 3 m from the source the dose rate becomes about', '["1/3 of the original", "1/6 of the original", "1/9 of the original", "the same, since gamma rays are very penetrating"]', 2, 'Radiation from a point source spreads out over a sphere, so intensity ∝ 1/r². Tripling the distance gives (1/3)² = 1/9 of the dose rate. Distance, shielding and short exposure time are the three ways to cut dose.', '{"0": "1/3 assumes intensity ∝ 1/r, not 1/r².", "1": "1/6 has no physical basis; intensity from a point source falls as 1/r².", "3": "Penetrating power does not stop the beam from spreading out with distance."}'),
  ('PHY-7008', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Medical uses of radiation', 'moderate', 'Physics 12 (PECTAA, Punjab), Ch. 19 Nuclear and particle physics (§19.6 Radioactive tracers, medical use); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics 13.4', 'Iodine-131 is used to treat an overactive thyroid gland because', '["iodine-131 emits only harmless visible light", "iodine-131 is not absorbed by any organ and passes straight out", "its half-life is millions of years, giving a steady dose", "the thyroid selectively absorbs iodine, so the radiation acts mainly on the thyroid tissue"]', 3, 'Radio-iodine behaves chemically like ordinary iodine, so it collects in the thyroid. Its beta and gamma radiation then destroy overactive or cancerous thyroid tissue, while its 8-day half-life keeps the exposure short.', '{"0": "Iodine-131 emits beta particles and gamma rays, which are ionizing.", "1": "Iodine is taken up strongly by the thyroid, which is the whole point of using it.", "2": "Iodine-131 has a half-life of about 8 days, not millions of years."}'),
  ('PHY-7009', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Medical uses of radiation', 'moderate', 'NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics 13.4 (biological effects and medical uses of radiation); not on the PMDC MDCAT 2025 syllabus', 'Gamma rays are used to sterilize sealed packs of syringes and surgical instruments because they', '["penetrate the packaging and kill microorganisms without heating the contents", "make the instruments radioactive so that germs cannot grow", "are absorbed completely by the outer wrapping", "only work on metal objects"]', 0, 'Gamma rays pass through plastic wrapping and kill bacteria by damaging their DNA. Items can be sterilized after sealing and without heat, so heat-sensitive plastics are safe; the items do not become radioactive.', '{"1": "Gamma irradiation does not make items radioactive.", "2": "If the wrapping absorbed all the rays, the contents would not be sterilized.", "3": "Plastic syringes, dressings and even food are sterilized this way."}'),
  ('PHY-7010', 'Current electricity', 'Electric power and kWh', 'moderate', 'NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics 7 (electric power, kilowatt-hour); PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 9', 'A household uses a 1.5 kW kettle for 20 minutes a day and a 60 W bulb for 5 hours a day. How much electrical energy do these use in 30 days?', '["15 kWh", "24 kWh", "9 kWh", "18 kWh"]', 1, 'Kettle: 1.5 kW × (1/3) h = 0.5 kWh per day. Bulb: 0.06 kW × 5 h = 0.3 kWh per day. Total 0.8 kWh × 30 = 24 kWh.', '{"0": "15 kWh counts only the kettle.", "2": "9 kWh counts only the bulb.", "3": "18 kWh treats 20 minutes as 0.2 h instead of 1/3 h."}'),
  ('PHY-7011', 'Current electricity', 'Electric power and kWh', 'hard', 'NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics 7 (electric power); PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 9', 'A heater is rated 1000 W at 220 V. If it is run on 110 V and its resistance stays the same, its power will be', '["500 W", "1000 W", "250 W", "125 W"]', 2, 'R = V²/P = 220²/1000 = 48.4 Ω. On 110 V, P = 110²/48.4 = 250 W. Halving the voltage quarters the power because P = V²/R.', '{"0": "500 W assumes power is proportional to voltage.", "1": "The power depends on the voltage applied, so it cannot stay 1000 W.", "3": "125 W divides by 8, i.e. assumes P ∝ V³."}'),
  ('PHY-7012', 'Current electricity', 'Electric power and kWh', 'easy', 'NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics 7 (kilowatt-hour); PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 9', 'Electricity costs Rs 50 per kWh. What does it cost to run a 2.5 kW air conditioner for 8 hours?', '["Rs 400", "Rs 2000", "Rs 125", "Rs 1000"]', 3, 'Energy = 2.5 kW × 8 h = 20 kWh. Cost = 20 × Rs 50 = Rs 1000.', '{"0": "Rs 400 multiplies 50 by 8 only, forgetting the 2.5 kW.", "1": "Rs 2000 doubles the energy used.", "2": "Rs 125 multiplies 2.5 by 50 only, forgetting the 8 hours."}'),
  ('PHY-7013', 'Force and motion', 'Rate of change of momentum', 'moderate', 'PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics 2.13 (second law as rate of change of momentum); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics 1.13', 'A 0.5 kg ball hits a wall at 12 m s⁻¹ along the normal and rebounds at 8 m s⁻¹. It is in contact with the wall for 0.05 s. The average force on the ball is', '["200 N", "40 N", "80 N", "400 N"]', 0, 'Taking the rebound direction as positive, Δp = 0.5 × 8 − 0.5 × (−12) = 10 kg m s⁻¹. F = Δp/Δt = 10/0.05 = 200 N.', '{"1": "40 N uses the change in speed 12 − 8 = 4 m s⁻¹, ignoring the reversal of direction.", "2": "80 N uses only the rebound momentum (0.5 × 8), not the full change.", "3": "400 N halves the contact time."}'),
  ('PHY-7014', 'Force and motion', 'Rate of change of momentum', 'hard', 'PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics 2.13 (second law as rate of change of momentum); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics 1.13', 'Sand falls vertically onto a horizontal conveyor belt at 2 kg s⁻¹. The belt moves at a steady 3 m s⁻¹. Ignoring friction in the rollers, the extra horizontal force needed to keep the belt moving at the same speed is', '["1.5 N", "6 N", "0 N, since the belt''s speed does not change", "18 N"]', 1, 'Each second, 2 kg of sand must be given a horizontal velocity of 3 m s⁻¹. F = rate of change of momentum = v × (Δm/Δt) = 3 × 2 = 6 N. This is a case where F = ma alone fails, because the mass changes while the velocity stays constant.', '{"0": "1.5 N divides speed by mass rate instead of multiplying.", "2": "The velocity of the belt is constant, but momentum is still being given to the new sand every second.", "3": "18 N multiplies by 3 twice."}'),
  ('PHY-7015', 'Force and motion', 'Rate of change of momentum', 'moderate', 'PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics 2.13 (second law as rate of change of momentum); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics 1.13', 'Newton''s second law written as F = Δp/Δt is more general than F = ma because it', '["applies only to bodies at rest", "does not need forces to be measured in newtons", "also applies when the mass of the system changes, as for a rocket", "shows that force and momentum are the same quantity"]', 2, 'F = ma follows from F = Δp/Δt only when m is constant. Rockets, conveyor belts and water jets involve changing mass, and only the momentum form gives the right force.', '{"0": "Bodies at rest have no change in momentum unless a net force acts; the law covers all motion.", "1": "Both forms use the newton.", "3": "Force is the rate of change of momentum, not momentum itself; they have different units."}'),
  ('PHY-7016', 'Force and motion', 'Rate of change of momentum', 'hard', 'PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics 2.13 (second law as rate of change of momentum); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics 1.13', 'A rocket of total mass 300 kg sits on its launch pad. Its exhaust gas leaves at 1500 m s⁻¹ relative to the rocket. What is the minimum rate at which it must eject gas to just lift off? (g = 10 m s⁻²)', '["0.2 kg s⁻¹", "20 kg s⁻¹", "5 kg s⁻¹", "2 kg s⁻¹"]', 3, 'Thrust = (mass ejected per second) × (exhaust speed). For lift-off, thrust must at least equal the weight 3000 N, so Δm/Δt = 3000/1500 = 2 kg s⁻¹.', '{"0": "0.2 kg s⁻¹ is ten times too small; it forgets g.", "1": "20 kg s⁻¹ uses a weight of 30 000 N.", "2": "5 kg s⁻¹ divides 1500 by 300."}'),
  ('PHY-7017', 'Force and motion', 'Rate of change of momentum', 'easy', 'PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics 2.13 (second law as rate of change of momentum); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics 1.13', 'The area under a force-time graph for a body equals its', '["change in momentum (impulse)", "change in kinetic energy", "work done", "acceleration"]', 0, 'Area = F × Δt = Δp. This is Newton''s second law in its momentum form, summed over time.', '{"1": "Change in kinetic energy is the area under a force-displacement graph.", "2": "Work done is also the area under a force-displacement graph.", "3": "Acceleration is read from a velocity-time graph."}'),
  ('PHY-7018', 'Force and motion', 'Launch angles with the same range', 'hard', 'PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics 2.11 (projectile range, time of flight, launch angles); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics 1.11', 'Two balls are launched with the same speed at 30° and 60° above the horizontal and land at the same point on level ground. The ratio of their times of flight t₃₀ : t₆₀ is', '["1 : 1", "1 : √3", "1 : 2", "√3 : 1"]', 1, 'Complementary angles give equal ranges, but time of flight T = 2u sinθ/g, so t₃₀/t₆₀ = sin 30°/sin 60° = (1/2)/(√3/2) = 1/√3.', '{"0": "Equal ranges do not mean equal times; the higher ball stays up longer.", "2": "1 : 2 is the ratio of the angles, not of their sines.", "3": "√3 : 1 inverts the ratio; the 60° ball takes longer."}'),
  ('PHY-7019', 'Force and motion', 'Launch angles with the same range', 'hard', 'PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics 2.11 (projectile range, time of flight, launch angles); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics 1.11', 'Two projectiles launched with the same speed at 30° and 60° have equal ranges. The ratio of their maximum heights H₃₀ : H₆₀ is', '["1 : √3", "1 : 2", "1 : 3", "1 : 1"]', 2, 'H = u² sin²θ/2g, so H₃₀/H₆₀ = sin²30°/sin²60° = (1/4)/(3/4) = 1/3.', '{"0": "1 : √3 is the ratio of the times of flight (or of sin θ), not of sin²θ.", "1": "1 : 2 is the ratio of the angles.", "3": "The heights differ even though the ranges are equal."}'),
  ('PHY-7020', 'Force and motion', 'Launch angles with the same range', 'hard', 'PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics 2.11 (projectile range, time of flight, launch angles); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics 1.11', 'A projectile has the same range R for launch angles θ and (90° − θ) at the same speed. If the times of flight are t₁ and t₂, then t₁t₂ equals', '["R/g", "R/2g", "4R/g", "2R/g"]', 3, 't₁t₂ = (2u sinθ/g)(2u cosθ/g) = 4u² sinθ cosθ/g² = 2(u² sin2θ/g)/g = 2R/g.', '{"0": "R/g misses the factor 2 from 4 sinθ cosθ = 2 sin 2θ.", "1": "R/2g is four times too small.", "2": "4R/g forgets that sin 2θ = 2 sinθ cosθ."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-7021', 'Force and motion', 'Speed of approach and separation', 'moderate', 'PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics 2.15–2.17 (collisions; relative speed of approach and separation); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics 1.15–1.17', 'Ball A moving at 2.0 m s⁻¹ catches up with ball B moving at 0.5 m s⁻¹ in the same direction along the same line. If the collision is perfectly elastic, the relative speed with which they separate is', '["1.5 m s⁻¹", "2.5 m s⁻¹", "2.0 m s⁻¹", "0.5 m s⁻¹"]', 0, 'Speed of approach = 2.0 − 0.5 = 1.5 m s⁻¹. In a perfectly elastic collision the relative speed of separation equals the relative speed of approach, whatever the masses.', '{"1": "2.5 m s⁻¹ adds the speeds, as if the balls were moving towards each other.", "2": "2.0 m s⁻¹ uses only A''s speed.", "3": "0.5 m s⁻¹ uses only B''s speed."}'),
  ('PHY-7022', 'Force and motion', 'Speed of approach and separation', 'moderate', 'PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics 2.15–2.17 (collisions; relative speed of approach and separation); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics 1.15–1.17', 'In a head-on collision, the two bodies separate at half the speed with which they approached. The collision is', '["perfectly elastic", "inelastic, with some kinetic energy lost", "perfectly inelastic", "one in which momentum is not conserved"]', 1, 'A perfectly elastic collision has equal speeds of approach and separation; a perfectly inelastic one has zero separation speed (the bodies stick). Anything in between is inelastic: momentum is conserved but some kinetic energy is lost.', '{"0": "Perfectly elastic would need the separation speed to equal the approach speed.", "2": "Perfectly inelastic bodies move together, so their speed of separation is zero.", "3": "Momentum is conserved in every collision of an isolated system."}'),
  ('PHY-7023', 'Force and motion', 'Speed of approach and separation', 'hard', 'PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics 2.15–2.17 (collisions; relative speed of approach and separation); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics 1.15–1.17', 'A 1 kg ball moving at 6 m s⁻¹ collides head-on and perfectly elastically with a 2 kg ball at rest. Their velocities after the collision are', '["+2 m s⁻¹ and +2 m s⁻¹", "0 and +3 m s⁻¹", "−2 m s⁻¹ (rebounds) and +4 m s⁻¹", "−3 m s⁻¹ and +4.5 m s⁻¹"]', 2, 'v₁ = (m₁ − m₂)u/(m₁ + m₂) = (−1/3) × 6 = −2 m s⁻¹; v₂ = 2m₁u/(m₁ + m₂) = (2/3) × 6 = 4 m s⁻¹. Check: momentum 6 = −2 + 8; separation speed 4 − (−2) = 6 = approach speed.', '{"0": "+2 and +2: the balls move together, which is a perfectly inelastic result (KE lost).", "1": "0 and +3 conserves momentum but loses half the kinetic energy; separation speed 3 ≠ 6.", "3": "−3 and +4.5 conserves momentum but the separation speed 7.5 m s⁻¹ is greater than 6, so kinetic energy would increase."}'),
  ('PHY-7024', 'Force and motion', 'Projectile from a height', 'moderate', 'PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics 2.11 (projectile launched from a height); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics 1.11', 'A stone is thrown horizontally at 15 m s⁻¹ from the top of a cliff 45 m high. How far from the foot of the cliff does it land? (g = 10 m s⁻²)', '["30 m", "90 m", "67.5 m", "45 m"]', 3, 'Vertical: 45 = ½ × 10 × t², so t = 3 s. Horizontal velocity stays 15 m s⁻¹, so x = 15 × 3 = 45 m.', '{"0": "30 m uses t = 2 s.", "1": "90 m uses t = 6 s (forgets the ½ in ½gt²).", "2": "67.5 m multiplies 45 by 1.5."}'),
  ('PHY-7025', 'Force and motion', 'Projectile from a height', 'hard', 'PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics 2.11 (projectile launched from a height); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics 1.11', 'A stone thrown horizontally at 15 m s⁻¹ from a 45 m high cliff hits the ground. The angle its velocity makes with the horizontal at impact is (g = 10 m s⁻²)', '["tan⁻¹ 2 (about 63°)", "45°", "tan⁻¹ ½ (about 27°)", "tan⁻¹ 3 (about 72°)"]', 0, 't = 3 s, so the vertical velocity is gt = 30 m s⁻¹ while the horizontal is still 15 m s⁻¹. tanθ = 30/15 = 2.', '{"1": "45° would need equal horizontal and vertical velocity components.", "2": "tan⁻¹ ½ inverts the ratio (horizontal over vertical).", "3": "tan⁻¹ 3 divides the fall height (45 m) by the horizontal speed (15 m s⁻¹)."}'),
  ('PHY-7026', 'Force and motion', 'Projectile from a height', 'hard', 'PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics 2.11 (projectile launched from a height); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics 1.11', 'A ball is thrown at 20 m s⁻¹ at 30° above the horizontal from the top of a 25 m high tower. How long does it take to reach the ground? (g = 10 m s⁻²)', '["2.00 s", "3.45 s", "2.24 s", "4.45 s"]', 1, 'Vertical component = 20 sin 30° = 10 m s⁻¹ upward. Taking up as positive: −25 = 10t − 5t², so t² − 2t − 5 = 0, t = 1 + √6 ≈ 3.45 s.', '{"0": "2.00 s is only the time to rise and come back to the launch height.", "2": "2.24 s (√5 s) is the time to fall 25 m from rest, ignoring the upward start.", "3": "4.45 s takes the root of t² − 2t − 5 = 0 wrongly as 2 + √6."}'),
  ('PHY-7027', 'Work and energy', 'Reference level for potential energy', 'moderate', 'PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics 3.5 (gravitational PE from a reference level; absolute PE); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics 2.5', 'A table top 0.8 m above the floor is chosen as the zero of gravitational potential energy. What is the potential energy of a 2 kg ball lying on the floor? (g = 10 m s⁻²)', '["+16 J", "0 J", "−16 J", "−30 J"]', 2, 'PE = mgh with h measured from the reference level. The floor is 0.8 m below it, so h = −0.8 m and PE = 2 × 10 × (−0.8) = −16 J.', '{"0": "+16 J ignores that the ball is below the reference level.", "1": "0 J would be right only if the floor were the reference level.", "3": "−30 J has no basis in the data."}'),
  ('PHY-7028', 'Work and energy', 'Absolute potential energy', 'hard', 'PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics 3.5 (gravitational PE from a reference level; absolute PE); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics 2.5', 'The gravitational potential energy of a satellite at distance r from Earth''s centre is U = −GMm/r. When the satellite is moved to a higher orbit, its potential energy', '["decreases, becoming more negative", "stays the same", "becomes positive", "increases, becoming less negative"]', 3, 'With zero at infinity, U = −GMm/r. A larger r makes U less negative, so U increases; work has to be done to lift the satellite.', '{"0": "More negative would mean moving closer to Earth.", "1": "U depends on r, so it changes.", "2": "U reaches zero only at infinity, so it stays negative at any finite distance."}'),
  ('PHY-7101', 'Vectors and equilibrium', 'Addition of vectors', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 1 (Vectors and equilibrium)', 'Two forces of equal magnitude F act at a point. Their resultant also has magnitude F. The angle between the two forces is', '["120°", "60°", "90°", "150°"]', 0, 'R² = F² + F² + 2F² cosθ. Setting R = F gives 2F²(1 + cosθ) = F², so cosθ = −½ and θ = 120°.', '{"1": "At 60° the resultant is √3 F.", "2": "At 90° the resultant is √2 F.", "3": "At 150° the resultant is about 0.52 F."}'),
  ('PHY-7102', 'Vectors and equilibrium', 'Addition of vectors', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 1 (Vectors and equilibrium)', 'The resultant of two forces P and Q (Q > P) is perpendicular to P. The magnitude of the resultant is', '["√(P² + Q²)", "√(Q² − P²)", "Q − P", "P + Q"]', 1, 'Take P along x. For the resultant to be perpendicular to P, its x-component must vanish: P + Q cosθ = 0. Then R = Q sinθ and R² = Q² − Q²cos²θ = Q² − P².', '{"0": "√(P² + Q²) is the resultant when P and Q themselves are perpendicular.", "2": "Q − P is the resultant when the forces are opposite, and then it lies along P.", "3": "P + Q is the resultant when the forces are parallel."}'),
  ('PHY-7103', 'Vectors and equilibrium', 'Scalar and vector products', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 1 (Vectors and equilibrium)', 'For two vectors A and B, |A × B| = √3 (A · B). The angle between A and B is', '["30°", "45°", "60°", "90°"]', 2, 'AB sinθ = √3 AB cosθ, so tanθ = √3 and θ = 60°.', '{"0": "At 30°, tanθ = 1/√3, so |A × B| would be (1/√3)(A · B).", "1": "At 45° the two products are equal.", "3": "At 90° the dot product is zero, so the relation cannot hold."}'),
  ('PHY-7104', 'Vectors and equilibrium', 'Scalar and vector products', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 1 (Vectors and equilibrium)', 'The vectors A = 2î + 3ĵ − k̂ and B = î − 2ĵ + c k̂ are perpendicular. The value of c is', '["4", "−8", "8", "−4"]', 3, 'Perpendicular vectors have zero dot product: 2(1) + 3(−2) + (−1)(c) = 0, so 2 − 6 − c = 0 and c = −4.', '{"0": "c = 4 drops the minus sign of the k̂ component of A.", "1": "c = −8 takes the y-product as +6 and also flips the sign.", "2": "c = 8 takes the y-product 3 × (−2) as +6."}'),
  ('PHY-7105', 'Vectors and equilibrium', 'Addition of vectors', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 1 (Vectors and equilibrium)', 'Two vectors of equal magnitude satisfy |A + B| = √3 |A − B|. The angle between them is', '["60°", "30°", "45°", "90°"]', 0, '|A + B|² = 2A²(1 + cosθ) and |A − B|² = 2A²(1 − cosθ). So 1 + cosθ = 3(1 − cosθ), cosθ = ½, θ = 60°.', '{"1": "At 30° the ratio |A + B|/|A − B| is about 3.7.", "2": "At 45° the ratio is about 2.4.", "3": "At 90° the sum and difference have equal magnitudes."}'),
  ('PHY-7106', 'Vectors and equilibrium', 'Equilibrium and torque', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 1 (Vectors and equilibrium)', 'A uniform horizontal beam 2 m long and weighing 200 N is hinged to a wall at one end. A vertical rope attached 1.5 m from the hinge holds it level. The tension in the rope is', '["100 N", "133 N", "150 N", "267 N"]', 1, 'Take moments about the hinge (so the hinge force has no moment): T × 1.5 = 200 × 1.0 (weight acts at the centre). T = 133 N.', '{"0": "100 N would be right if the rope were at the free end, 2 m from the hinge.", "2": "150 N uses 200 × 1.5/2.", "3": "267 N uses 200 × 2/1.5, placing the weight at the far end."}'),
  ('PHY-7107', 'Vectors and equilibrium', 'Equilibrium and torque', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 1 (Vectors and equilibrium)', 'A 10 kg lamp hangs from two cords, each making 30° with the horizontal ceiling. The tension in each cord is (g = 10 m s⁻²)', '["50 N", "57.7 N", "100 N", "200 N"]', 2, 'Vertical balance: 2T sin 30° = 100 N, so T = 100 N. Shallow cords need large tensions.', '{"0": "50 N simply halves the weight, as if the cords were vertical.", "1": "57.7 N uses cos 30°, i.e. treats the angle as measured from the vertical.", "3": "200 N forgets that there are two cords."}'),
  ('PHY-7108', 'Vectors and equilibrium', 'Scalar and vector products', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 1 (Vectors and equilibrium)', 'The area of the parallelogram whose adjacent sides are A = 3î + 4ĵ and B = −2î + 5ĵ is', '["7 units", "11.5 units", "26.9 units", "23 units"]', 3, 'Area = |A × B| = |3 × 5 − 4 × (−2)| = 15 + 8 = 23.', '{"0": "7 drops the minus sign of −2 (15 − 8).", "1": "11.5 is the area of the triangle formed by A and B.", "2": "26.9 is |A||B| = 5 × √29, which would need the vectors to be perpendicular."}'),
  ('PHY-7109', 'Vectors and equilibrium', 'Scalar and vector products', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 1 (Vectors and equilibrium)', 'The component of A = 4î + 3ĵ along the direction of B = î + ĵ is', '["4.95", "7", "3.5", "9.9"]', 0, 'Component = A · B̂ = (4 + 3)/|B| = 7/√2 ≈ 4.95.', '{"1": "7 is A · B, not divided by |B| = √2.", "2": "3.5 divides by 2 instead of √2.", "3": "9.9 multiplies by √2 instead of dividing."}'),
  ('PHY-7110', 'Vectors and equilibrium', 'Scalar and vector products', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 1 (Vectors and equilibrium)', 'A force F = (3î + 4ĵ) N moves a body from the point (1, 2) m to the point (4, −2) m. The work done by the force is', '["25 J", "−7 J", "7 J", "−25 J"]', 1, 'Displacement d = (4 − 1)î + (−2 − 2)ĵ = 3î − 4ĵ. W = F · d = 9 − 16 = −7 J.', '{"0": "25 J adds the products as if the y-displacement were +4.", "2": "7 J has the right size but the wrong sign.", "3": "−25 J has no basis in F · d."}'),
  ('PHY-7111', 'Force and motion', 'Free fall', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 2 (Force and motion); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 1', 'A body falls freely from rest. The distance it covers in the last second of its fall equals the distance it covered in the first 3 s. The total time of fall is (g = 10 m s⁻²)', '["4 s", "6 s", "5 s", "9 s"]', 2, 'First 3 s: ½ × 10 × 9 = 45 m. Distance in the t-th second = (g/2)(2t − 1) = 5(2t − 1). Setting 5(2t − 1) = 45 gives t = 5 s.', '{"0": "In the 4th second the body falls 35 m.", "1": "In the 6th second it falls 55 m.", "3": "9 s comes from setting 5t = 45."}'),
  ('PHY-7112', 'Force and motion', 'Uniform acceleration', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 2 (Force and motion); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 1', 'A body starts from rest with uniform acceleration. The ratio of the distance covered in the 3rd second to the distance covered in the first 3 seconds is', '["1 : 3", "9 : 5", "3 : 5", "5 : 9"]', 3, 'In the n-th second, s = (a/2)(2n − 1) = 5a/2 for n = 3. In the first 3 s, s = ½a(3)² = 9a/2. Ratio 5 : 9.', '{"0": "1 : 3 is 3a/2 : 9a/2, using the 2nd second instead of the 3rd.", "1": "9 : 5 inverts the ratio.", "2": "3 : 5 compares the 2nd and 3rd seconds."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-7113', 'Force and motion', 'Velocity-time graph', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 2 (Force and motion); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 1', 'A car starts from rest, accelerates at 2 m s⁻² for 10 s, moves at constant speed for 20 s, then slows uniformly to rest in 5 s. The total distance travelled is', '["550 m", "500 m", "600 m", "450 m"]', 0, 'Area under the v-t graph: ½ × 10 × 20 = 100 m, then 20 × 20 = 400 m, then ½ × 5 × 20 = 50 m. Total 550 m.', '{"1": "500 m leaves out the 50 m braking stage.", "2": "600 m uses 10 s instead of 5 s for braking.", "3": "450 m leaves out the 100 m covered while accelerating."}'),
  ('PHY-7114', 'Force and motion', 'Average velocity', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 2 (Force and motion); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 1', 'A car covers the first half of a journey at 40 km h⁻¹ and the second half at 60 km h⁻¹. Its average speed for the whole journey is', '["50 km h⁻¹", "48 km h⁻¹", "45 km h⁻¹", "52 km h⁻¹"]', 1, 'For distance 2d: time = d/40 + d/60 = d/24. Average speed = 2d ÷ (d/24) = 48 km h⁻¹. Equal distances (not equal times) give the harmonic mean, which is below 50.', '{"0": "50 km h⁻¹ is the simple mean, valid only for equal times at each speed.", "2": "45 km h⁻¹ has no basis in the data.", "3": "52 km h⁻¹ would need more time at the higher speed."}'),
  ('PHY-7115', 'Force and motion', 'Motion under gravity', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 2 (Force and motion); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 1', 'A ball is thrown straight up at 30 m s⁻¹. Two seconds later a second ball is thrown up from the same point, also at 30 m s⁻¹. How long after the first throw do they meet? (g = 10 m s⁻²)', '["3 s", "5 s", "4 s", "2 s"]', 2, 'Heights: y₁ = 30t − 5t², y₂ = 30(t − 2) − 5(t − 2)². Equating: 0 = −60 + 20t − 20, so t = 4 s (both at 40 m; the first ball is falling, the second rising).', '{"0": "At 3 s the first ball is at its top (45 m) and the second is only 25 m up.", "1": "At 5 s the first ball is at 25 m and the second at 45 m.", "3": "At 2 s the second ball has only just been thrown."}'),
  ('PHY-7116', 'Force and motion', 'Projectile motion', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 2 (Force and motion); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 1', 'At the highest point of its path, the speed of a projectile is half its launch speed. The launch angle is', '["30°", "45°", "75°", "60°"]', 3, 'At the top only the horizontal component u cosθ remains. u cosθ = u/2 gives θ = 60°.', '{"0": "At 30° the top speed would be 0.87u.", "1": "At 45° it would be 0.71u.", "2": "At 75° it would be 0.26u."}'),
  ('PHY-7117', 'Force and motion', 'Projectile motion', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 2 (Force and motion); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 1', 'A projectile''s maximum height equals its horizontal range. Its launch angle θ is given by', '["tan θ = 4", "tan θ = ¼", "θ = 45°", "tan θ = 2"]', 0, 'H/R = (u² sin²θ/2g) ÷ (2u² sinθ cosθ/g) = tanθ/4. Setting H = R gives tanθ = 4 (about 76°).', '{"1": "tanθ = ¼ would make H/R = 1/16.", "2": "At 45°, H = R/4.", "3": "tanθ = 2 gives H = R/2."}'),
  ('PHY-7118', 'Force and motion', 'Projectile motion', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 2 (Force and motion); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 1', 'A projectile is launched at 20 m s⁻¹ at 60° above the horizontal. After what time is its velocity perpendicular to its initial velocity? (g = 10 m s⁻²)', '["1.73 s", "2.31 s", "3.46 s", "4.00 s"]', 1, 'v · u = 0: u² − (u sinθ)gt = 0, so t = u/(g sinθ) = 20/(10 × 0.866) = 2.31 s. This is before landing (time of flight 3.46 s), so it really happens.', '{"0": "1.73 s is the time to reach the top, where the velocity is horizontal, not perpendicular to u.", "2": "3.46 s is the whole time of flight.", "3": "4.00 s uses sin 30° instead of sin 60°."}'),
  ('PHY-7119', 'Force and motion', 'Projectile motion', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 2 (Force and motion); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 1', 'For which launch angle can a projectile''s velocity, at some moment during its flight over level ground, be perpendicular to its initial velocity?', '["20°", "35°", "60°", "40°"]', 2, 'The velocity is perpendicular to u at t = u/(g sinθ). This must be no later than landing at 2u sinθ/g, which needs sin²θ ≥ ½, i.e. θ ≥ 45°. Of the choices only 60° qualifies.', '{"0": "At 20° the required time is longer than the flight, so it lands first.", "1": "At 35° it lands before the velocity can turn through 90°.", "3": "At 40° it still lands just before; 45° is the minimum."}'),
  ('PHY-7120', 'Force and motion', 'Projectile motion', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 2 (Force and motion); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 1', 'The greatest horizontal range a gun can reach is 80 m. Fired straight up with the same muzzle speed, the bullet would rise to (no air resistance)', '["80 m", "20 m", "160 m", "40 m"]', 3, 'Maximum range R = u²/g (at 45°) = 80 m. Straight up, H = u²/2g = R/2 = 40 m.', '{"0": "80 m equates the vertical height to the maximum range.", "1": "20 m is the maximum height reached when fired at 45°.", "2": "160 m doubles instead of halves."}'),
  ('PHY-7121', 'Force and motion', 'Newton''s laws', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 2 (Force and motion); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 1', 'A 60 kg person stands on a weighing scale in a lift that is moving upward but slowing down at 2 m s⁻². The scale reads (g = 10 m s⁻²)', '["480 N", "600 N", "720 N", "120 N"]', 0, 'Slowing down while moving up means the acceleration is downward, 2 m s⁻². N = m(g − a) = 60 × 8 = 480 N. The direction of motion does not matter; only the acceleration does.', '{"1": "600 N is the reading with no acceleration.", "2": "720 N would be for an upward acceleration of 2 m s⁻².", "3": "120 N is only ma."}'),
  ('PHY-7122', 'Force and motion', 'Newton''s laws', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 2 (Force and motion); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 1', 'Masses of 3 kg and 2 kg hang from the two ends of a light string over a frictionless pulley. The tension in the string is (g = 10 m s⁻²)', '["20 N", "24 N", "30 N", "25 N"]', 1, 'a = (3 − 2)g/(3 + 2) = 2 m s⁻². For the 2 kg mass, T − 20 = 2 × 2, so T = 24 N (check with 3 kg: 30 − T = 6).', '{"0": "20 N is the weight of the 2 kg mass; the string must pull harder than that to accelerate it upward.", "2": "30 N is the weight of the 3 kg mass, which would give it no acceleration.", "3": "25 N is the average of the weights."}'),
  ('PHY-7123', 'Force and motion', 'Newton''s laws', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 2 (Force and motion); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 1', 'A 12 N horizontal force pushes a 2 kg block, which pushes a 4 kg block in front of it on a frictionless floor. The force the 2 kg block exerts on the 4 kg block is', '["4 N", "12 N", "8 N", "6 N"]', 2, 'a = 12/(2 + 4) = 2 m s⁻². The only horizontal force on the 4 kg block is the contact force, so it is 4 × 2 = 8 N.', '{"0": "4 N is the net force on the 2 kg block (12 − 8).", "1": "12 N would leave the 2 kg block with no net force.", "3": "6 N splits the force equally regardless of mass."}'),
  ('PHY-7124', 'Force and motion', 'Newton''s laws', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 2 (Force and motion); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 1', 'A 4 kg block on a horizontal table (μk = 0.2) is tied by a string over a light frictionless pulley to a hanging 1 kg mass. Once moving, the acceleration of the system is (g = 10 m s⁻²)', '["2.0 m s⁻²", "1.6 m s⁻²", "0", "0.4 m s⁻²"]', 3, 'Driving force = 1 × 10 = 10 N. Friction = 0.2 × 40 = 8 N. a = (10 − 8)/(4 + 1) = 0.4 m s⁻².', '{"0": "2.0 m s⁻² ignores friction.", "1": "1.6 m s⁻² divides 8 N by 5 kg.", "2": "The pull (10 N) is more than the friction (8 N), so there is a net force."}'),
  ('PHY-7125', 'Force and motion', 'Inelastic collision', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 2 (Force and motion); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 1', 'A 50 g bullet travelling at 400 m s⁻¹ embeds itself in a 1.95 kg wooden block at rest on a frictionless surface. What fraction of the bullet''s kinetic energy is lost?', '["97.5%", "2.5%", "50%", "75%"]', 0, 'Common velocity v = (0.05 × 400)/2.0 = 10 m s⁻¹. KE before = ½ × 0.05 × 400² = 4000 J; after = ½ × 2.0 × 10² = 100 J. Lost 3900/4000 = 97.5% (fraction = M/(m + M)).', '{"1": "2.5% is the fraction that remains, not the fraction lost.", "2": "50% is the loss only when the two masses are equal.", "3": "75% has no basis in the data."}'),
  ('PHY-7126', 'Force and motion', 'Momentum and explosions', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 2 (Force and motion); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 1', 'A 3 kg object at rest explodes into two pieces of 1 kg and 2 kg. The 1 kg piece moves off at 20 m s⁻¹. The total kinetic energy released is', '["200 J", "300 J", "400 J", "600 J"]', 1, 'Momentum: 1 × 20 = 2 × v, so v = 10 m s⁻¹. KE = ½ × 1 × 400 + ½ × 2 × 100 = 200 + 100 = 300 J.', '{"0": "200 J counts only the 1 kg piece.", "2": "400 J gives the 2 kg piece 20 m s⁻¹ as well, breaking momentum conservation.", "3": "600 J doubles the right answer."}'),
  ('PHY-7127', 'Force and motion', 'Momentum and explosions', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 2 (Force and motion); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 1', 'A 4 kg gun fires a 20 g bullet at 300 m s⁻¹. The kinetic energy of the gun''s recoil is', '["900 J", "6 J", "4.5 J", "9 J"]', 2, 'Recoil speed V = (0.02 × 300)/4 = 1.5 m s⁻¹. KE = ½ × 4 × 1.5² = 4.5 J. The momenta are equal but the light bullet takes almost all the energy (900 J).', '{"0": "900 J is the bullet''s kinetic energy.", "1": "6 J is the gun''s momentum in kg m s⁻¹, not an energy.", "3": "9 J forgets the ½."}'),
  ('PHY-7128', 'Force and motion', 'Elastic collision', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 2 (Force and motion); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 1', 'A ball of mass m moving at v collides head-on and elastically with a stationary ball of mass 3m. What fraction of its kinetic energy does it transfer?', '["1/4", "1/2", "1/3", "3/4"]', 3, 'v₂ = 2m v/(4m) = v/2. KE of 3m = ½(3m)(v/2)² = (3/8)mv², which is 3/4 of ½mv². (Fraction = 4m₁m₂/(m₁ + m₂)².)', '{"0": "1/4 is the fraction the first ball keeps (it rebounds at v/2).", "1": "1/2 assumes the energy is shared equally.", "2": "1/3 is the mass ratio, not the energy fraction."}'),
  ('PHY-7129', 'Force and motion', 'Elastic collision', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 2 (Force and motion); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 1', 'A 2 kg ball moving at 3 m s⁻¹ collides head-on and elastically with a 1 kg ball moving at 3 m s⁻¹ towards it. After the collision, the 1 kg ball moves at', '["5 m s⁻¹ in the original direction of the 2 kg ball", "3 m s⁻¹ in the original direction of the 2 kg ball", "1 m s⁻¹ in the original direction of the 2 kg ball", "6 m s⁻¹ in the original direction of the 2 kg ball"]', 0, 'Momentum: 2(3) + 1(−3) = 3 = 2v₁ + v₂. Elastic: v₂ − v₁ = 6. So 3v₁ = −3, v₁ = −1 m s⁻¹ and v₂ = 5 m s⁻¹.', '{"1": "3 m s⁻¹ would need the 2 kg ball to stop, losing kinetic energy, which an elastic collision cannot do.", "2": "1 m s⁻¹ is the speed of the 2 kg ball after the collision (it rebounds).", "3": "6 m s⁻¹ is the relative speed, not the ball''s velocity."}'),
  ('PHY-7130', 'Force and motion', 'Impulse', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 2 (Force and motion); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 1', 'A net force on a 2 kg body at rest rises steadily from 0 to 10 N in 2 s, then falls steadily back to 0 at t = 4 s. The speed of the body at t = 4 s is', '["20 m s⁻¹", "10 m s⁻¹", "5 m s⁻¹", "40 m s⁻¹"]', 1, 'Impulse = area of the triangle = ½ × 4 × 10 = 20 N s = Δp. v = 20/2 = 10 m s⁻¹.', '{"0": "20 m s⁻¹ takes the impulse (20 N s) as the speed, forgetting to divide by the mass.", "2": "5 m s⁻¹ halves again.", "3": "40 m s⁻¹ uses the rectangle 10 × 4 and forgets the mass."}'),
  ('PHY-7131', 'Force and motion', 'Velocity-time graph', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 2 (Force and motion); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 1', 'A body''s velocity changes uniformly from +20 m s⁻¹ to −12 m s⁻¹ in 8 s. The distance it travels (not its displacement) in these 8 s is', '["32 m", "50 m", "68 m", "80 m"]', 2, 'a = −32/8 = −4 m s⁻². It stops at t = 5 s after covering ½ × 5 × 20 = 50 m, then moves back for 3 s, covering ½ × 3 × 12 = 18 m. Distance = 68 m; displacement = 32 m.', '{"0": "32 m is the displacement (50 − 18).", "1": "50 m counts only the forward motion.", "3": "80 m is ½ × 20 × 8, as if the body slowed to rest over the whole 8 s."}'),
  ('PHY-7132', 'Force and motion', 'Projectile motion', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 2 (Force and motion); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 1', 'From the top of a building, ball A is thrown horizontally at speed u and ball B is thrown straight down at the same speed u. Ignoring air resistance,', '["both land together with the same speed", "A lands first and with the greater speed", "B hits the ground with the greater speed", "both hit the ground with the same speed, but B lands first"]', 3, 'Energy: v² = u² + 2gh for both, so their impact speeds are equal. But B starts with a downward velocity u while A starts with zero vertical velocity, so B reaches the ground first.', '{"0": "They cannot land together: their vertical starting velocities differ.", "1": "A takes longer, since it starts with no downward speed; its impact speed equals B''s.", "2": "Both gain the same kinetic energy mgh from the same starting speed."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-7133', 'Force and motion', 'Friction', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 2 (Force and motion); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 1', 'A 5 kg box rests on a floor with μs = 0.5 and μk = 0.4. A horizontal push of 20 N is applied. The friction force on the box is (g = 10 m s⁻²)', '["20 N", "25 N", "0", "5 N"]', 0, 'Limiting static friction = 0.5 × 50 = 25 N. The 20 N push is less than this, so the box stays at rest and static friction exactly balances it: 20 N.', '{"1": "25 N is only the maximum static friction; friction is never more than needed.", "2": "A push is acting, so friction must oppose it.", "3": "5 N is 25 − 20, which is not a force acting on the box."}'),
  ('PHY-7134', 'Force and motion', 'Newton''s laws', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 2 (Force and motion); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 1', 'A 1000 kg car pulls a 500 kg trailer with an acceleration of 2 m s⁻². Resistive forces are 300 N on the car and 200 N on the trailer. The tension in the tow bar is', '["1000 N", "1200 N", "1167 N", "3500 N"]', 1, 'Trailer alone: T − 200 = 500 × 2, so T = 1200 N. (The car''s driving force is 1500 × 2 + 500 = 3500 N.)', '{"0": "1000 N ignores the resistance on the trailer.", "2": "1167 N shares the 3500 N driving force in proportion to mass, ignoring where the resistances act.", "3": "3500 N is the driving force on the whole system."}'),
  ('PHY-7135', 'Work and energy', 'Work against friction', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 3 (Work and energy); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 2', 'A 2 kg block starts from rest and slides down a rough slope 5 m high, reaching the bottom at 8 m s⁻¹. The energy dissipated as heat by friction is (g = 10 m s⁻²)', '["64 J", "100 J", "36 J", "164 J"]', 2, 'Loss of PE = 2 × 10 × 5 = 100 J. Gain of KE = ½ × 2 × 64 = 64 J. Work against friction = 100 − 64 = 36 J.', '{"0": "64 J is the kinetic energy gained.", "1": "100 J is the potential energy lost.", "3": "164 J adds the two instead of subtracting."}'),
  ('PHY-7136', 'Work and energy', 'Power', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 3 (Work and energy); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 2', 'A pump raises 600 kg of water per minute through 20 m and delivers it at 10 m s⁻¹. Its output power is (g = 10 m s⁻²)', '["2000 W", "500 W", "150 000 W", "2500 W"]', 3, 'Per second: 10 kg. PE rate = 10 × 10 × 20 = 2000 W; KE rate = ½ × 10 × 100 = 500 W. Total 2500 W.', '{"0": "2000 W ignores the kinetic energy given to the water.", "1": "500 W counts only the kinetic energy.", "2": "150 000 W is the energy per minute, not per second."}'),
  ('PHY-7137', 'Work and energy', 'Power as F · v', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 3 (Work and energy); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 2', 'A 1000 kg car climbs a slope with sinθ = 0.1 at a steady 20 m s⁻¹ against a resistance of 500 N. The power it delivers is (g = 10 m s⁻²)', '["30 kW", "10 kW", "20 kW", "200 kW"]', 0, 'Driving force = mg sinθ + resistance = 1000 + 500 = 1500 N. P = Fv = 1500 × 20 = 30 kW.', '{"1": "10 kW counts only the resistance.", "2": "20 kW counts only the slope component of weight.", "3": "200 kW uses the full weight mg instead of mg sinθ."}'),
  ('PHY-7138', 'Work and energy', 'Efficiency', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 3 (Work and energy); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 2', 'A motor of efficiency 80% lifts 400 kg through 12 m at steady speed in 30 s. Its electrical input power is (g = 10 m s⁻²)', '["1600 W", "2000 W", "1280 W", "1920 W"]', 1, 'Useful power = mgh/t = 400 × 10 × 12/30 = 1600 W. Input = 1600/0.8 = 2000 W.', '{"0": "1600 W is the useful (output) power.", "2": "1280 W multiplies by 0.8 instead of dividing.", "3": "1920 W adds 20% of 1600."}'),
  ('PHY-7139', 'Work and energy', 'Work by a variable force', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 3 (Work and energy); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 2', 'A force on a body rises linearly from 0 to 20 N as it moves 4 m, then stays at 20 N for the next 2 m. The total work done is', '["120 J", "40 J", "80 J", "60 J"]', 2, 'Work = area under the F-x graph: triangle ½ × 4 × 20 = 40 J plus rectangle 20 × 2 = 40 J, total 80 J.', '{"0": "120 J treats the force as 20 N throughout.", "1": "40 J counts only one part.", "3": "60 J uses an average force of 10 N over 6 m."}'),
  ('PHY-7140', 'Work and energy', 'Kinetic energy and momentum', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 3 (Work and energy); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 2', 'If the momentum of a body increases by 20%, its kinetic energy increases by', '["20%", "40%", "21%", "44%"]', 3, 'KE = p²/2m, so KE ∝ p². (1.2)² = 1.44, an increase of 44%.', '{"0": "20% treats KE as proportional to p.", "1": "40% doubles the percentage, ignoring the square of 0.2.", "2": "21% squares 1.1, as if the momentum had risen by only 10%."}'),
  ('PHY-7141', 'Work and energy', 'Kinetic energy and momentum', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 3 (Work and energy); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 2', 'Bodies of mass 1 kg and 4 kg have equal kinetic energies. The ratio of their momenta p₁ : p₂ is', '["1 : 2", "1 : 4", "2 : 1", "1 : 16"]', 0, 'p = √(2mK). With equal K, p ∝ √m, so p₁/p₂ = √(1/4) = 1/2.', '{"1": "1 : 4 treats p as proportional to m.", "2": "2 : 1 inverts the ratio; the heavier body has the larger momentum.", "3": "1 : 16 squares the mass ratio."}'),
  ('PHY-7142', 'Work and energy', 'Conservation of energy', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 3 (Work and energy); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 2', 'A 0.5 kg pendulum bob on a 2 m string is released from rest with the string horizontal. The tension in the string as the bob passes the lowest point is (g = 10 m s⁻²)', '["5 N", "15 N", "10 N", "20 N"]', 1, 'Energy: v² = 2gL = 40 m² s⁻². At the bottom, T − mg = mv²/L, so T = 5 + 0.5 × 40/2 = 15 N, i.e. 3mg whatever the length.', '{"0": "5 N is just the weight; the bob also needs a centripetal force.", "2": "10 N is only the centripetal force mv²/L.", "3": "20 N (4mg) adds the weight twice to the centripetal force."}'),
  ('PHY-7143', 'Work and energy', 'Absolute potential energy', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 3 (Work and energy); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 2', 'Taking g = 10 m s⁻² and Earth''s radius R = 6.4 × 10⁶ m, the minimum energy needed to move a 1000 kg probe from Earth''s surface to infinity (ignoring air and Earth''s spin) is', '["3.2 × 10¹⁰ J", "6.4 × 10⁷ J", "6.4 × 10¹⁰ J", "1.28 × 10¹¹ J"]', 2, 'Absolute PE at the surface is −GMm/R = −mgR (since GM = gR²). Raising it to zero at infinity needs mgR = 1000 × 10 × 6.4 × 10⁶ = 6.4 × 10¹⁰ J.', '{"0": "3.2 × 10¹⁰ J is the energy to reach a height R, not infinity.", "1": "6.4 × 10⁷ J uses R in km.", "3": "1.28 × 10¹¹ J doubles the answer."}'),
  ('PHY-7144', 'Work and energy', 'Absolute potential energy', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 3 (Work and energy); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 2', 'The increase in gravitational potential energy when a body of mass m is raised from Earth''s surface to a height equal to Earth''s radius R is', '["mgR", "2mgR", "mgR/4", "mgR/2"]', 3, 'ΔU = −GMm/2R − (−GMm/R) = GMm/2R = mgR/2. Using mgh = mgR overestimates it because g weakens with height.', '{"0": "mgR assumes g stays constant all the way up.", "1": "2mgR has no basis.", "2": "mgR/4 uses the field at height R, g/4, as if it acted over the whole climb."}'),
  ('PHY-7145', 'Work and energy', 'Work against friction', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 3 (Work and energy); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 2', 'A 1200 kg car moving at 72 km h⁻¹ skids to rest with its wheels locked. If μk = 0.5, the skid distance is (g = 10 m s⁻²)', '["40 m", "20 m", "80 m", "518 m"]', 0, '72 km h⁻¹ = 20 m s⁻¹. Friction work = KE: μmg d = ½mv², so d = v²/(2μg) = 400/10 = 40 m (independent of mass).', '{"1": "20 m uses v²/(4μg), doubling the denominator.", "2": "80 m forgets the ½ in the kinetic energy.", "3": "518 m uses 72 instead of 20 m s⁻¹."}'),
  ('PHY-7146', 'Work and energy', 'Energy losses', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 3 (Work and energy); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 2', 'A ball dropped from 20 m rebounds to 12.8 m. The fraction of its kinetic energy lost in the impact, and the ratio of its speeds just after and just before impact, are', '["64% lost; speed ratio 0.64", "36% lost; speed ratio 0.8", "36% lost; speed ratio 0.64", "20% lost; speed ratio 0.8"]', 1, 'KE ∝ height reached, so 12.8/20 = 0.64 of the KE remains: 36% is lost. Speed ∝ √KE, so the speed ratio is √0.64 = 0.8.', '{"0": "64% is what remains, not what is lost, and 0.64 is the energy ratio.", "2": "0.64 is the energy ratio; the speed ratio is its square root.", "3": "20% is the fall in speed, not in energy."}'),
  ('PHY-7147', 'Work and energy', 'Power', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 3 (Work and energy); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 2', 'A 2000 kg car accelerates uniformly from rest to 20 m s⁻¹ in 10 s on a level road. Ignoring friction, its average power output is', '["80 kW", "20 kW", "40 kW", "4 kW"]', 2, 'Average power = ΔKE/t = (½ × 2000 × 400)/10 = 40 kW. (The power at the end, F × v = 4000 × 20 = 80 kW, is twice the average because power rises with speed.)', '{"0": "80 kW is the instantaneous power at 20 m s⁻¹.", "1": "20 kW halves again.", "3": "4 kW is the driving force in kN, not a power."}'),
  ('PHY-7148', 'Work and energy', 'Work-energy theorem', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 3 (Work and energy); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 2', 'A 10 g bullet enters a 10 cm thick plank at 500 m s⁻¹ and leaves at 300 m s⁻¹. The average resistive force of the plank is', '["800 N", "4000 N", "16000 N", "8000 N"]', 3, 'Loss of KE = ½ × 0.01 × (500² − 300²) = 800 J. F × 0.1 m = 800 J, so F = 8000 N.', '{"0": "800 N forgets to divide by 0.1 m (it is the energy in joules).", "1": "4000 N uses (500 − 300)² instead of 500² − 300².", "2": "16000 N forgets the ½ in KE."}'),
  ('PHY-7149', 'Circular motion', 'Angular displacement', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 4 (Circular motion); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 3', 'A wheel starts from rest with a constant angular acceleration of 2 rad s⁻². How many revolutions does it make in 10 s?', '["15.9", "100", "31.8", "50"]', 0, 'θ = ½αt² = ½ × 2 × 100 = 100 rad. Revolutions = 100/2π ≈ 15.9.', '{"1": "100 is the angle in radians, not revolutions.", "2": "31.8 divides by π instead of 2π.", "3": "50 forgets to convert and halves."}'),
  ('PHY-7150', 'Circular motion', 'Linear and angular acceleration', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 4 (Circular motion); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 3', 'A flywheel of radius 0.5 m speeds up uniformly from 120 rpm to 360 rpm in 4 s. The tangential acceleration of a point on its rim is', '["6.28 m s⁻²", "3.14 m s⁻²", "1.57 m s⁻²", "12.6 m s⁻²"]', 1, 'ω₁ = 4π, ω₂ = 12π rad s⁻¹. α = 8π/4 = 2π rad s⁻². aₜ = rα = 0.5 × 2π = π ≈ 3.14 m s⁻².', '{"0": "6.28 m s⁻² is α in rad s⁻², not multiplied by r.", "2": "1.57 m s⁻² halves again.", "3": "12.6 m s⁻² (4π) multiplies α by 2 instead of by r = 0.5 m."}'),
  ('PHY-7151', 'Circular motion', 'Centripetal force', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 4 (Circular motion); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 3', 'A car takes a flat curve of radius 50 m. The coefficient of static friction between tyres and road is 0.8. The maximum safe speed is (g = 10 m s⁻²)', '["40 m s⁻¹", "14.1 m s⁻¹", "20 m s⁻¹", "28.3 m s⁻¹"]', 2, 'Friction supplies the centripetal force: mv²/r ≤ μmg, so v ≤ √(μgr) = √(0.8 × 10 × 50) = 20 m s⁻¹.', '{"0": "40 m s⁻¹ is √(4μgr), double the correct speed.", "1": "14.1 m s⁻¹ is √(0.4 × 10 × 50), halving μ.", "3": "28.3 m s⁻¹ is √(gr × 1.6), doubling μ."}'),
  ('PHY-7152', 'Circular motion', 'Centripetal force', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 4 (Circular motion); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 3', 'A curve of radius 90 m is banked so that cars at 15 m s⁻¹ need no friction. The banking angle θ is (g = 10 m s⁻²)', '["tan⁻¹ 0.5 (about 27°)", "45°", "tan⁻¹ 4 (about 76°)", "tan⁻¹ 0.25 (about 14°)"]', 3, 'For no friction, tanθ = v²/(rg) = 225/900 = 0.25, so θ ≈ 14°.', '{"0": "tan⁻¹ 0.5 would need a radius of 45 m.", "1": "45° would need v² = rg, i.e. 30 m s⁻¹.", "2": "tan⁻¹ 4 inverts the ratio."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-7153', 'Circular motion', 'Vertical circle', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 4 (Circular motion); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 3', 'A stone on a string is whirled in a vertical circle of radius 0.9 m. The minimum speed it must have at the lowest point to just complete the circle is (g = 10 m s⁻²)', '["6.7 m s⁻¹", "3.0 m s⁻¹", "4.2 m s⁻¹", "9.0 m s⁻¹"]', 0, 'At the top the minimum speed is √(gr) (tension just zero). Energy from bottom to top: v_b² = v_t² + 4gr = 5gr = 45, so v_b = 6.7 m s⁻¹.', '{"1": "3.0 m s⁻¹ is √(gr), the minimum speed at the top.", "2": "4.2 m s⁻¹ is √(2gr), enough only to reach the height of the centre.", "3": "9.0 m s⁻¹ (√(9gr)) would complete the circle but is not the minimum."}'),
  ('PHY-7154', 'Circular motion', 'Vertical circle', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 4 (Circular motion); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 3', 'A ball on a string moves in a vertical circle. Whatever its speed, the tension at the lowest point exceeds the tension at the highest point by', '["2mg", "6mg", "4mg", "5mg"]', 1, 'Bottom: T_b = mg + mv_b²/r. Top: T_t = mv_t²/r − mg. Energy: v_b² − v_t² = 4gr. So T_b − T_t = 2mg + m(4gr)/r = 6mg.', '{"0": "2mg counts only the weight terms, forgetting the speed difference.", "2": "4mg counts only the speed difference.", "3": "5mg confuses this with v_b² = 5gr for the minimum speed."}'),
  ('PHY-7155', 'Circular motion', 'Linear and angular velocity', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 4 (Circular motion); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 3', 'Earth turns once in 24 h and its equatorial radius is 6.4 × 10⁶ m. The linear speed of a point on the equator due to this rotation is about', '["233 m s⁻¹", "930 m s⁻¹", "465 m s⁻¹", "74 m s⁻¹"]', 2, 'ω = 2π/86 400 = 7.27 × 10⁻⁵ rad s⁻¹. v = ωR = 7.27 × 10⁻⁵ × 6.4 × 10⁶ ≈ 465 m s⁻¹.', '{"0": "233 m s⁻¹ uses π instead of 2π.", "1": "930 m s⁻¹ uses 4π.", "3": "74 m s⁻¹ forgets the 2π (R/86 400)."}'),
  ('PHY-7156', 'Circular motion', 'Centripetal acceleration', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 4 (Circular motion); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 3', 'A disc rotates at 300 rpm. The centripetal acceleration of a point 10 cm from its axis is about', '["3.14 m s⁻²", "9.87 m s⁻²", "987 m s⁻²", "98.7 m s⁻²"]', 3, 'ω = 300 × 2π/60 = 10π rad s⁻¹. a = ω²r = 100π² × 0.1 ≈ 98.7 m s⁻² (about 10g).', '{"0": "3.14 m s⁻² is ωr, the linear speed, not the acceleration.", "1": "9.87 m s⁻² uses ω = π√10, dropping a factor.", "2": "987 m s⁻² uses r = 1 m."}'),
  ('PHY-7157', 'Circular motion', 'Orbital motion', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 4 (Circular motion); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 3', 'A satellite orbits just above Earth''s surface. Taking g = 10 m s⁻² and R = 6.4 × 10⁶ m, its orbital speed is', '["8.0 km s⁻¹", "11.3 km s⁻¹", "5.7 km s⁻¹", "64 km s⁻¹"]', 0, 'Gravity provides the centripetal force: mg = mv²/R, so v = √(gR) = √(6.4 × 10⁷) = 8000 m s⁻¹.', '{"1": "11.3 km s⁻¹ is √(2gR), the escape speed.", "2": "5.7 km s⁻¹ is √(gR/2).", "3": "64 km s⁻¹ forgets the square root: gR = 6.4 × 10⁷ m² s⁻², misread as a speed."}'),
  ('PHY-7158', 'Circular motion', 'Change in velocity in circular motion', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 4 (Circular motion); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 3', 'The second hand of a clock is 2 cm long. The magnitude of the change in velocity of its tip in 15 s is about', '["zero, since its speed is constant", "2.96 × 10⁻³ m s⁻¹", "2.09 × 10⁻³ m s⁻¹", "4.19 × 10⁻³ m s⁻¹"]', 1, 'Speed v = 2π × 0.02/60 = 2.09 × 10⁻³ m s⁻¹. In 15 s the velocity turns through 90°, so |Δv| = v√2 = 2.96 × 10⁻³ m s⁻¹.', '{"0": "The speed is constant but the direction changes, so velocity changes.", "2": "2.09 × 10⁻³ m s⁻¹ is the speed itself.", "3": "4.19 × 10⁻³ m s⁻¹ is 2v, the change over half a turn (30 s)."}'),
  ('PHY-7159', 'Circular motion', 'Angular displacement', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 4 (Circular motion); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 3', 'A runner goes a quarter of the way round a circular track of radius 100 m. The angular displacement and the magnitude of the linear displacement are', '["π/2 rad and 157 m", "90 rad and 141 m", "π/2 rad and 141 m", "π/4 rad and 100 m"]', 2, 'A quarter turn is π/2 rad. The arc length is 157 m, but the displacement is the straight chord between the two radii at 90°: 100√2 ≈ 141 m.', '{"0": "157 m is the arc length (distance), not the displacement.", "1": "90 is the angle in degrees, not radians.", "3": "π/4 rad is an eighth of a turn."}'),
  ('PHY-7160', 'Circular motion', 'Centripetal force', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 4 (Circular motion); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 3', 'A coin placed 10 cm from the centre of a turntable just begins to slip when the table turns at 60 rpm. Placed 40 cm from the centre, it would start to slip at', '["15 rpm", "120 rpm", "42 rpm", "30 rpm"]', 3, 'Slipping starts when ω²r = μg, so ω ∝ 1/√r. Four times the radius halves ω: 60/2 = 30 rpm.', '{"0": "15 rpm assumes ω ∝ 1/r.", "1": "120 rpm makes slipping harder farther out, which is backwards.", "2": "42 rpm uses 60/√2."}'),
  ('PHY-7161', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Equation of continuity', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 5 (Fluid dynamics)', 'Water flows at 2 m s⁻¹ through a pipe of diameter 4 cm, which narrows to a diameter of 2 cm. The speed in the narrow section is', '["8 m s⁻¹", "4 m s⁻¹", "1 m s⁻¹", "16 m s⁻¹"]', 0, 'A₁v₁ = A₂v₂ and A ∝ d². Halving the diameter quarters the area, so the speed becomes 4 × 2 = 8 m s⁻¹.', '{"1": "4 m s⁻¹ treats the area as proportional to the diameter.", "2": "1 m s⁻¹ makes the fluid slow down in the narrow part.", "3": "16 m s⁻¹ squares the area ratio again (as if A ∝ d⁴)."}'),
  ('PHY-7162', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Bernoulli''s equation', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 5 (Fluid dynamics)', 'Water flows along a horizontal pipe. Its speed rises from 2 m s⁻¹ to 6 m s⁻¹ at a constriction. The drop in pressure is (density 1000 kg m⁻³)', '["32 000 Pa", "16 000 Pa", "8 000 Pa", "18 000 Pa"]', 1, 'For a horizontal pipe, P₁ − P₂ = ½ρ(v₂² − v₁²) = ½ × 1000 × (36 − 4) = 16 000 Pa.', '{"0": "32 000 Pa forgets the ½.", "2": "8 000 Pa uses ½ρ(v₂ − v₁)² = ½ × 1000 × 16.", "3": "18 000 Pa uses only v₂² (½ × 1000 × 36)."}'),
  ('PHY-7163', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Torricelli''s theorem', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 5 (Fluid dynamics)', 'An open tank holds water to a height of 2 m above the ground. A small hole is made in its side at height h above the ground. For which h does the jet land farthest from the tank?', '["0.5 m", "1.5 m", "1.0 m", "2.0 m"]', 2, 'Efflux speed v = √(2g(H − h)); fall time t = √(2h/g). Range x = vt = 2√(h(H − h)), which is greatest when h = H/2 = 1 m (x = 2 m).', '{"0": "At 0.5 m the range is 2√(0.75) ≈ 1.73 m.", "1": "At 1.5 m the range is also about 1.73 m.", "3": "At 2.0 m (the surface) there is no pressure head and no jet."}'),
  ('PHY-7164', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Terminal velocity', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 5 (Fluid dynamics)', 'A small steel ball falls through oil at a terminal velocity of 2 cm s⁻¹. A ball of the same steel with twice the radius would have a terminal velocity of', '["4 cm s⁻¹", "2.8 cm s⁻¹", "16 cm s⁻¹", "8 cm s⁻¹"]', 3, 'At terminal velocity, weight (minus upthrust) ∝ r³ balances the Stokes drag 6πηrv. So v ∝ r², and doubling r gives 4 × 2 = 8 cm s⁻¹.', '{"0": "4 cm s⁻¹ assumes v ∝ r.", "1": "2.8 cm s⁻¹ assumes v ∝ √r.", "2": "16 cm s⁻¹ assumes v ∝ r³, forgetting that drag also grows with r."}'),
  ('PHY-7165', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Terminal velocity', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 5 (Fluid dynamics)', 'Eight identical raindrops, each falling at terminal velocity v, merge into one larger drop. Its terminal velocity is', '["4v", "8v", "2v", "v"]', 0, 'Volume is conserved: 8 × (4/3)πr³ = (4/3)πR³, so R = 2r. Terminal velocity ∝ radius², so the new velocity is 4v.', '{"1": "8v assumes the velocity grows with volume.", "2": "2v assumes v ∝ r.", "3": "Larger drops fall faster; the velocity does change."}'),
  ('PHY-7166', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Fluid drag', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 5 (Fluid dynamics)', 'A sphere of radius 1.0 mm moves at 0.10 m s⁻¹ through glycerine of viscosity 1.5 Pa s. The viscous drag on it (Stokes''s law) is', '["9.0 × 10⁻⁴ N", "2.8 × 10⁻³ N", "2.8 × 10⁻⁶ N", "1.4 × 10⁻³ N"]', 1, 'F = 6πηrv = 6π × 1.5 × 1.0 × 10⁻³ × 0.10 ≈ 2.8 × 10⁻³ N.', '{"0": "9.0 × 10⁻⁴ N leaves out the π.", "2": "2.8 × 10⁻⁶ N takes the radius as 1 µm.", "3": "1.4 × 10⁻³ N uses 3π instead of 6π."}'),
  ('PHY-7167', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Equation of continuity', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 5 (Fluid dynamics)', 'Blood flows through an aorta of radius 1.0 cm at an average speed of 30 cm s⁻¹. The volume flow rate is about', '["30 cm³ s⁻¹", "9.4 cm³ s⁻¹", "94 cm³ s⁻¹", "188 cm³ s⁻¹"]', 2, 'Flow rate = Av = πr²v = π × 1.0² × 30 ≈ 94 cm³ s⁻¹.', '{"0": "30 cm³ s⁻¹ forgets the area of the aorta.", "1": "9.4 cm³ s⁻¹ is ten times too small (a units slip).", "3": "188 cm³ s⁻¹ uses 2πr instead of πr²."}'),
  ('PHY-7168', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Equation of continuity', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 5 (Fluid dynamics)', 'Blood leaves the aorta (cross-section 3 cm²) at 30 cm s⁻¹ and later passes through capillaries whose total cross-section is 2000 cm². The average speed in the capillaries is', '["0.45 cm s⁻¹", "20 cm s⁻¹", "0.0045 cm s⁻¹", "0.045 cm s⁻¹"]', 3, 'Total flow is conserved: 3 × 30 = 2000 × v, so v = 90/2000 = 0.045 cm s⁻¹. Each capillary is narrow, but together they have a much larger area, so blood moves slowly there, giving time for exchange.', '{"0": "0.45 cm s⁻¹ is ten times too large.", "1": "20 cm s⁻¹ assumes blood speeds up in narrow vessels; it is the total area that counts.", "2": "0.0045 cm s⁻¹ is ten times too small."}'),
  ('PHY-7169', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Bernoulli''s effect in blood flow', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 5 (Fluid dynamics)', 'Compared with a normal part of an artery, blood in a narrowed (partly blocked) segment', '["flows faster and exerts a lower pressure on the artery wall", "flows slower and exerts a higher pressure", "flows faster and exerts a higher pressure", "flows at the same speed and pressure"]', 0, 'Continuity makes the blood speed up in the narrower part; Bernoulli then requires its pressure to fall. The low pressure can let the outside tissue squeeze the artery further (vascular flutter).', '{"1": "A narrower section makes the blood speed up, not slow down.", "2": "Higher speed goes with lower pressure (Bernoulli), not higher.", "3": "The same volume per second through a smaller area means a higher speed."}'),
  ('PHY-7170', 'Fluid dynamics', 'Laminar and turbulent flow', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 5 (Fluid dynamics)', 'Which change makes the flow of a liquid through a pipe more likely to become turbulent?', '["Increasing the viscosity of the liquid", "Increasing the speed of flow", "Using a narrower pipe at the same speed", "Using a less dense liquid"]', 1, 'Flow turns turbulent when the ratio ρvD/η (the Reynolds number) is large. Higher speed raises it; higher viscosity, a narrower pipe or a lower density all lower it.', '{"0": "Higher viscosity damps out eddies and keeps flow laminar.", "2": "A smaller diameter at the same speed lowers ρvD/η.", "3": "Lower density lowers ρvD/η."}'),
  ('PHY-7171', 'Waves', 'Wave speed and phase', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 6 (Waves); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 4', 'A sound wave of frequency 500 Hz travels at 340 m s⁻¹. The phase difference between two points 17 cm apart along its path is', '["π", "π/4", "π/2", "2π"]', 2, 'λ = v/f = 0.68 m. Δφ = 2π × Δx/λ = 2π × 0.17/0.68 = π/2.', '{"0": "π would need the points to be λ/2 = 34 cm apart.", "1": "π/4 would need 8.5 cm.", "3": "2π would need a full wavelength, 68 cm."}'),
  ('PHY-7172', 'Waves', 'Speed of sound and temperature', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 6 (Waves); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 4', 'The speed of sound in air is 347 m s⁻¹ at 27 °C. At 127 °C it is about', '["463 m s⁻¹", "347 m s⁻¹", "1634 m s⁻¹", "401 m s⁻¹"]', 3, 'v ∝ √T (kelvin): v = 347 × √(400/300) ≈ 401 m s⁻¹.', '{"0": "463 m s⁻¹ takes v ∝ T instead of √T.", "1": "Speed of sound does depend on temperature.", "2": "1634 m s⁻¹ uses the Celsius ratio 127/27 directly."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-7173', 'Waves', 'Laplace correction', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 6 (Waves); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 4', 'Newton''s formula v = √(P/ρ) gives 280 m s⁻¹ for air. Taking γ = 1.4 for air, Laplace''s corrected value is about', '["331 m s⁻¹", "392 m s⁻¹", "280 m s⁻¹", "237 m s⁻¹"]', 0, 'Sound compressions are adiabatic, so v = √(γP/ρ) = 280 × √1.4 ≈ 331 m s⁻¹.', '{"1": "392 m s⁻¹ multiplies by γ instead of √γ.", "2": "280 m s⁻¹ is Newton''s uncorrected (isothermal) value.", "3": "237 m s⁻¹ divides by √1.4."}'),
  ('PHY-7174', 'Waves', 'Factors affecting the speed of sound', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 6 (Waves); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 4', 'If the pressure of the air is doubled while its temperature is kept constant, the speed of sound in it', '["doubles", "stays the same", "increases by a factor √2", "halves"]', 1, 'v = √(γP/ρ). At constant temperature, doubling P also doubles ρ (Boyle''s law), so P/ρ and the speed are unchanged. Only temperature, humidity and the type of gas matter.', '{"0": "Doubling would need P/ρ to rise four times.", "2": "√2 would follow only if ρ stayed fixed, which it does not.", "3": "Higher pressure does not slow sound."}'),
  ('PHY-7175', 'Waves', 'Stationary waves in strings', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 6 (Waves); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 4', 'A string 0.80 m long fixed at both ends vibrates in four loops at 500 Hz. The speed of waves on the string is', '["400 m s⁻¹", "100 m s⁻¹", "200 m s⁻¹", "800 m s⁻¹"]', 2, 'Each loop is λ/2, so 4 × λ/2 = 0.80 m and λ = 0.40 m. v = fλ = 500 × 0.40 = 200 m s⁻¹.', '{"0": "400 m s⁻¹ takes λ = 0.80 m, the whole string length.", "1": "100 m s⁻¹ takes λ = 0.20 m (each loop as λ).", "3": "800 m s⁻¹ takes λ = 1.6 m."}'),
  ('PHY-7176', 'Waves', 'Stationary waves in strings', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 6 (Waves); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 4', 'A stretched string has a fundamental frequency of 200 Hz. If its tension is increased by 44%, the new fundamental frequency is', '["288 Hz", "220 Hz", "167 Hz", "240 Hz"]', 3, 'f = (1/2L)√(T/μ), so f ∝ √T. √1.44 = 1.2, giving 240 Hz.', '{"0": "288 Hz takes f ∝ T.", "1": "220 Hz uses only half the percentage increase.", "2": "167 Hz divides by 1.2, as if a tighter string vibrated more slowly."}'),
  ('PHY-7177', 'Waves', 'Organ pipes', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 6 (Waves); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 4', 'The fundamental frequency of a closed pipe equals the frequency of the first overtone of an open pipe 60 cm long. The length of the closed pipe is', '["15 cm", "30 cm", "7.5 cm", "60 cm"]', 0, 'Open pipe first overtone (2nd harmonic) = 2 × v/2L = v/0.60. Closed pipe fundamental = v/4L_c. Equating v/4L_c = v/0.60 gives L_c = 0.60/4 m = 15 cm.', '{"1": "30 cm matches the open pipe''s fundamental instead.", "2": "7.5 cm uses 8L_c.", "3": "60 cm equates the pipes directly."}'),
  ('PHY-7178', 'Waves', 'Organ pipes', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 6 (Waves); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 4', 'A pipe closed at one end has a fundamental frequency of 100 Hz. Which of these frequencies can it NOT produce?', '["300 Hz", "200 Hz", "500 Hz", "700 Hz"]', 1, 'A closed pipe has a node at the closed end and an antinode at the open end, so only odd harmonics occur: 100, 300, 500, 700 Hz… The even harmonic 200 Hz is missing.', '{"0": "300 Hz is the 3rd harmonic, which a closed pipe can produce.", "2": "500 Hz is the 5th harmonic.", "3": "700 Hz is the 7th harmonic."}'),
  ('PHY-7179', 'Waves', 'Interference of sound', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 6 (Waves); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 4', 'Two loudspeakers driven in phase emit sound of wavelength 0.68 m. At a point P, the path difference from the two speakers is 1.70 m. At P there is', '["constructive interference (a loud spot)", "partial interference, half as loud", "destructive interference (a quiet spot)", "no interference, since sound waves cannot interfere"]', 2, 'Path difference / λ = 1.70/0.68 = 2.5, an odd number of half wavelengths. The waves arrive in antiphase and cancel.', '{"0": "Constructive interference needs a whole number of wavelengths.", "1": "2.5λ is an exact half-wavelength condition, giving cancellation, not half intensity.", "3": "Sound waves from coherent sources do interfere."}'),
  ('PHY-7180', 'Waves', 'Stationary waves', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 6 (Waves); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 4', 'In a stationary wave, the distance between a node and the next antinode is 5 cm and the frequency is 680 Hz. The speed of the travelling waves that form it is', '["68 m s⁻¹", "34 m s⁻¹", "272 m s⁻¹", "136 m s⁻¹"]', 3, 'Node to next antinode = λ/4, so λ = 20 cm = 0.20 m. v = fλ = 680 × 0.20 = 136 m s⁻¹.', '{"0": "68 m s⁻¹ takes node-to-antinode as λ/2.", "1": "34 m s⁻¹ takes it as a full wavelength.", "2": "272 m s⁻¹ takes it as λ/8."}'),
  ('PHY-7181', 'Waves', 'Stationary waves', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 6 (Waves); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 4', 'In a stationary wave, all particles of the medium between two adjacent nodes', '["vibrate in phase with each other but with different amplitudes", "vibrate with the same amplitude but different phases", "are at rest at all times", "vibrate with the same amplitude and in phase"]', 0, 'Between adjacent nodes every particle moves up and down together (in phase). The amplitude is zero at the nodes and largest at the antinode in the middle. Particles in the next segment move in antiphase.', '{"1": "Same amplitude with varying phase describes a travelling wave.", "2": "Only the nodes themselves are always at rest.", "3": "The amplitude varies from zero at a node to a maximum at the antinode."}'),
  ('PHY-7182', 'Waves', 'Energy transfer by waves', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 6 (Waves); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 4', 'The energy carried by a wave is proportional to (amplitude)² × (frequency)². If a wave''s amplitude is doubled and its frequency halved, the energy it carries', '["doubles", "stays the same", "becomes four times", "halves"]', 1, 'E ∝ A²f² → (2)² × (½)² = 4 × ¼ = 1, so the energy is unchanged.', '{"0": "\"Doubles\" squares the amplitude factor but not the frequency factor (4 × ½).", "2": "Four times counts only the amplitude change.", "3": "Halving counts only the frequency change, and only once."}'),
  ('PHY-7183', 'Waves', 'Doppler effect', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 6 (Waves); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 4', 'A source of 600 Hz moves away from a stationary observer at 34 m s⁻¹. The speed of sound is 340 m s⁻¹. The frequency heard is about', '["540 Hz", "660 Hz", "545 Hz", "667 Hz"]', 2, 'Source receding: f′ = f × v/(v + v_s) = 600 × 340/374 ≈ 545 Hz.', '{"0": "540 Hz uses f(v − v_s)/v, the formula for an observer moving away.", "1": "660 Hz is for an observer moving towards the source.", "3": "667 Hz is for the source approaching."}'),
  ('PHY-7184', 'Waves', 'Doppler effect', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 6 (Waves); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 4', 'Sound of 600 Hz travels at 340 m s⁻¹. In case 1 the source approaches a stationary listener at 34 m s⁻¹; in case 2 the listener approaches a stationary source at 34 m s⁻¹. The frequencies heard are', '["660 Hz in case 1 and 667 Hz in case 2", "660 Hz in both cases", "667 Hz in both cases", "667 Hz in case 1 and 660 Hz in case 2"]', 3, 'Case 1: f′ = 600 × 340/306 ≈ 667 Hz. Case 2: f′ = 600 × 374/340 = 660 Hz. The Doppler shift depends on which one moves relative to the air, not just their relative speed.', '{"0": "This swaps the two formulas.", "1": "Only the moving-listener case gives 660 Hz.", "2": "Only the moving-source case gives 667 Hz."}'),
  ('PHY-7185', 'Waves', 'Doppler effect', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 6 (Waves); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 4', 'A train sounding a 500 Hz whistle passes a stationary observer at 20 m s⁻¹. The speed of sound is 340 m s⁻¹. By how much does the frequency heard drop as the train passes?', '["59 Hz", "29 Hz", "0 Hz", "118 Hz"]', 0, 'Approaching: 500 × 340/320 = 531.3 Hz. Receding: 500 × 340/360 = 472.2 Hz. Drop ≈ 59 Hz.', '{"1": "29 Hz is only the rise above 500 Hz on approach (31 Hz) or fall on receding (28 Hz), not the total drop.", "2": "The pitch clearly drops as the train passes.", "3": "118 Hz counts the drop twice."}'),
  ('PHY-7186', 'Waves', 'Doppler effect', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 6 (Waves); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 4', 'A stationary listener hears a frequency 10% higher than that emitted by a source approaching her. If sound travels at 330 m s⁻¹, the source''s speed is', '["33 m s⁻¹", "30 m s⁻¹", "36 m s⁻¹", "300 m s⁻¹"]', 1, 'f′/f = v/(v − v_s) = 1.1, so 330 = 1.1(330 − v_s), giving v_s = 30 m s⁻¹.', '{"0": "33 m s⁻¹ (10% of v) is the speed an approaching listener would need.", "2": "36 m s⁻¹ has no basis in the formula.", "3": "300 m s⁻¹ is v − v_s, not the source speed v_s."}'),
  ('PHY-7187', 'Waves', 'Simple harmonic motion', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 6 (Waves); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 4', 'A particle performs SHM with amplitude 4 cm and period 2 s. Its speed when it is 2 cm from the mean position is', '["12.6 cm s⁻¹", "6.3 cm s⁻¹", "10.9 cm s⁻¹", "21.8 cm s⁻¹"]', 2, 'ω = 2π/T = π rad s⁻¹. v = ω√(A² − x²) = π√(16 − 4) = π√12 ≈ 10.9 cm s⁻¹.', '{"0": "12.6 cm s⁻¹ is the maximum speed ωA, reached at the mean position.", "1": "6.3 cm s⁻¹ is ωx = 2π, using x instead of √(A² − x²).", "3": "21.8 cm s⁻¹ doubles the right answer (ω = 2π)."}'),
  ('PHY-7188', 'Waves', 'Simple harmonic motion', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 6 (Waves); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 4', 'A body in SHM has a maximum speed of 0.6 m s⁻¹ and a maximum acceleration of 1.8 m s⁻². Its amplitude is', '["0.33 m", "3 m", "1.08 m", "0.2 m"]', 3, 'v_max = ωA and a_max = ω²A, so ω = a_max/v_max = 3 rad s⁻¹ and A = 0.6/3 = 0.2 m.', '{"0": "0.33 m is v_max/a_max, which has units of time.", "1": "3 is ω in rad s⁻¹, not the amplitude.", "2": "1.08 m is v_max × a_max."}'),
  ('PHY-7189', 'Waves', 'Simple pendulum', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 6 (Waves); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 4', 'A simple pendulum has a period of 2.0 s in a stationary lift. What is its period when the lift accelerates upward at g/3?', '["1.73 s", "2.31 s", "2.0 s", "1.5 s"]', 0, 'In an upward-accelerating lift the effective gravity is g + g/3 = 4g/3. T ∝ 1/√g_eff, so T = 2.0 × √(3/4) ≈ 1.73 s.', '{"1": "2.31 s inverts the factor, multiplying by √(4/3) instead of √(3/4).", "2": "The period changes because the effective g changes.", "3": "1.5 s takes T ∝ 1/g_eff."}'),
  ('PHY-7190', 'Waves', 'Mass-spring system', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 6 (Waves); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 4', 'A mass on a spring oscillates with period 2.0 s. The spring is cut in half and the same mass is hung from one half. The new period is', '["1.0 s", "1.41 s", "2.83 s", "4.0 s"]', 1, 'Half a spring is twice as stiff (k → 2k). T = 2π√(m/k) ∝ 1/√k, so T = 2.0/√2 ≈ 1.41 s.', '{"0": "1.0 s takes T ∝ 1/k.", "2": "2.83 s assumes the half spring is softer.", "3": "4.0 s assumes k halves and T ∝ 1/k."}'),
  ('PHY-7191', 'Waves', 'Energy in SHM', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 6 (Waves)', 'For a body in SHM of amplitude A, the kinetic and potential energies are equal when the displacement is', '["A/2", "A/4", "A/√2", "√3A/2"]', 2, 'PE = ½kx² and total E = ½kA². PE = KE means PE = E/2, so x² = A²/2 and x = A/√2.', '{"0": "At A/2 the PE is only a quarter of the total.", "1": "At A/4 the PE is 1/16 of the total.", "3": "At √3A/2 the PE is 3/4 of the total."}'),
  ('PHY-7192', 'Waves', 'SHM and circular motion', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 6 (Waves)', 'A point moves round a circle of radius 10 cm at 2 revolutions per second. The maximum acceleration of its projection on a diameter is about', '["1.26 m s⁻²", "3.95 m s⁻²", "158 m s⁻²", "15.8 m s⁻²"]', 3, 'The projection performs SHM with A = 0.10 m and ω = 2π × 2 = 4π rad s⁻¹. a_max = ω²A = 16π² × 0.10 ≈ 15.8 m s⁻².', '{"0": "1.26 m s⁻² is ωA, the maximum speed.", "1": "3.95 m s⁻² uses ω = 2π (1 revolution per second).", "2": "158 m s⁻² takes A = 1 m."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-7193', 'Waves', 'Simple harmonic motion', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 6 (Waves); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 4', 'A particle performs SHM, x = A sin(2πt/T), with period T = 12 s. The least time it takes to move from the mean position to x = A/2 is', '["1 s", "2 s", "3 s", "0.5 s"]', 0, 'A/2 = A sin(2πt/12) gives 2πt/12 = π/6, so t = 1 s (T/12). Going from A/2 on to A takes a further 2 s, because the particle slows down near the extreme.', '{"1": "2 s is T/6, the time from A/2 to the extreme.", "2": "3 s is T/4, the time from the mean position to the extreme.", "3": "0.5 s is T/24."}'),
  ('PHY-7194', 'Waves', 'Wave speed', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 6 (Waves); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 4', 'A 200 Hz sound passes from air (speed 340 m s⁻¹) into water (speed 1480 m s⁻¹). Its wavelength in water is', '["1.7 m", "7.4 m", "0.74 m", "0.39 m"]', 1, 'Frequency is set by the source and does not change at the boundary. λ = v/f = 1480/200 = 7.4 m (in air it was 1.7 m).', '{"0": "1.7 m is the wavelength in air.", "2": "0.74 m is ten times too small.", "3": "0.39 m assumes the wavelength shrinks by 340/1480 from 1.7 m."}'),
  ('PHY-7195', 'Thermodynamics', 'First law of thermodynamics', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 7 (Thermodynamics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 5', 'A gas expands at a constant pressure of 2.0 × 10⁵ Pa from 3.0 L to 5.0 L while absorbing 1000 J of heat. The change in its internal energy is', '["+1400 J", "+400 J", "+600 J", "+1000 J"]', 2, 'Work done by the gas W = PΔV = 2.0 × 10⁵ × 2.0 × 10⁻³ = 400 J. ΔU = Q − W = 1000 − 400 = +600 J.', '{"0": "1400 J adds the work instead of subtracting it.", "1": "400 J is the work done by the gas.", "3": "1000 J ignores the work done during expansion."}'),
  ('PHY-7196', 'Thermodynamics', 'First law of thermodynamics', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 7 (Thermodynamics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 5', 'An ideal gas expands isothermally and absorbs 500 J of heat. The work done by the gas is', '["0 J", "250 J", "1000 J", "500 J"]', 3, 'Isothermal: T is constant, so ΔU = 0 for an ideal gas. Q = ΔU + W gives W = Q = 500 J.', '{"0": "Zero work would mean no expansion.", "1": "250 J splits the heat between ΔU and W, but ΔU = 0 here.", "2": "1000 J doubles the heat supplied, breaking energy conservation."}'),
  ('PHY-7197', 'Thermodynamics', 'First law of thermodynamics', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 7 (Thermodynamics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 5', 'A gas is compressed adiabatically and 300 J of work is done on it. Its internal energy', '["increases by 300 J, so its temperature rises", "decreases by 300 J, so its temperature falls", "does not change, since no heat flows", "increases by 600 J"]', 0, 'Adiabatic: Q = 0. With work W done on the gas, ΔU = Q + W_on = +300 J. The gas warms up (as in a bicycle pump).', '{"1": "Work done on the gas adds energy; it does not remove it.", "2": "No heat flows, but work still changes the internal energy.", "3": "600 J double-counts the work."}'),
  ('PHY-7198', 'Thermodynamics', 'Cp − Cv = R', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 7 (Thermodynamics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 5', 'For a certain ideal gas, Cp = 29.1 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹. Its molar specific heat at constant volume is (R = 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹)', '["37.4 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹", "20.8 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹", "29.1 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹", "12.5 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹"]', 1, 'Cv = Cp − R = 29.1 − 8.31 ≈ 20.8 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹ (a diatomic gas, Cv = 5R/2).', '{"0": "37.4 adds R instead of subtracting it.", "2": "Cp and Cv differ by R for an ideal gas.", "3": "12.5 is 3R/2, Cv for a monatomic gas."}'),
  ('PHY-7199', 'Thermodynamics', 'Specific heat and molar specific heat', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 7 (Thermodynamics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 5', 'The specific heat of nitrogen at constant volume is 743 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹ and its molar mass is 0.028 kg mol⁻¹. Its molar specific heat at constant volume is', '["26 500 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹", "743 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹", "20.8 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹", "0.74 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹"]', 2, 'Molar specific heat = specific heat × molar mass = 743 × 0.028 ≈ 20.8 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹.', '{"0": "26 500 divides by the molar mass instead of multiplying.", "1": "743 is the specific heat per kilogram, not per mole.", "3": "0.74 converts to J g⁻¹ K⁻¹, which is not per mole."}'),
  ('PHY-7200', 'Thermodynamics', 'Molar specific heats', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 7 (Thermodynamics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 5', 'Two moles of a monatomic ideal gas are heated at constant volume through 10 K. The heat required is (R = 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹)', '["416 J", "166 J", "125 J", "249 J"]', 3, 'Monatomic Cv = 3R/2 = 12.5 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹. Q = nCvΔT = 2 × 12.5 × 10 ≈ 249 J.', '{"0": "416 J uses Cp = 5R/2, the constant-pressure value.", "1": "166 J uses R alone (nRΔT is the work at constant pressure).", "2": "125 J is for one mole."}'),
  ('PHY-7201', 'Thermodynamics', 'Work done by a gas', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 7 (Thermodynamics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 5', 'Two moles of an ideal gas are heated at constant pressure through 10 K. The work done by the gas is (R = 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹)', '["166 J", "249 J", "416 J", "83 J"]', 0, 'At constant pressure, W = PΔV = nRΔT = 2 × 8.31 × 10 ≈ 166 J, whatever kind of ideal gas it is.', '{"1": "249 J is the rise in internal energy for a monatomic gas.", "2": "416 J is the heat supplied to a monatomic gas.", "3": "83 J is for one mole."}'),
  ('PHY-7202', 'Thermodynamics', 'Molar specific heats', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 7 (Thermodynamics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 5', 'A diatomic ideal gas (Cv = 5R/2) is heated at constant pressure. What fraction of the heat supplied goes into increasing its internal energy?', '["2/7", "5/7", "3/5", "2/5"]', 1, 'Q = nCpΔT and ΔU = nCvΔT, so ΔU/Q = Cv/Cp = (5R/2)/(7R/2) = 5/7. The remaining 2/7 does work.', '{"0": "2/7 is the fraction that becomes work.", "2": "3/5 is Cv/Cp for a monatomic gas.", "3": "2/5 is the work fraction for a monatomic gas."}'),
  ('PHY-7203', 'Thermodynamics', 'First law of thermodynamics', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 7 (Thermodynamics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 5', 'A gas is taken once round a closed cycle and does 200 J of net work. The net heat it absorbs during the cycle is', '["0 J", "400 J", "200 J", "−200 J"]', 2, 'The gas returns to its starting state, so ΔU = 0 over the cycle. Q = ΔU + W = 200 J.', '{"0": "Zero heat would mean zero net work.", "1": "400 J double-counts.", "3": "−200 J would mean the gas gave out heat while doing work, which breaks energy conservation."}'),
  ('PHY-7204', 'Thermodynamics', 'Thermal equilibrium', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 7 (Thermodynamics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 5', '200 g of water at 80 °C is mixed with 300 g of water at 20 °C in an insulated container. The final temperature is', '["50 °C", "56 °C", "40 °C", "44 °C"]', 3, 'Heat lost = heat gained: 200(80 − θ) = 300(θ − 20), so 16 000 + 6000 = 500θ and θ = 44 °C.', '{"0": "50 °C is the simple average, which ignores the different masses.", "1": "56 °C weights the temperatures the wrong way round.", "2": "40 °C has no basis in the heat balance."}'),
  ('PHY-7205', 'Thermodynamics', 'Work done by a gas', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 7 (Thermodynamics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 5', 'A gas is taken from state A (1.0 × 10⁵ Pa, 1.0 L) to state B (3.0 × 10⁵ Pa, 3.0 L) along a straight line on a P-V diagram. The work done by the gas is', '["400 J", "200 J", "600 J", "800 J"]', 0, 'Work = area under the line = trapezium = ½(1.0 + 3.0) × 10⁵ × 2.0 × 10⁻³ = 400 J.', '{"1": "200 J uses only the lower pressure (rectangle under A).", "2": "600 J uses only the higher pressure.", "3": "800 J forgets the ½."}'),
  ('PHY-7206', 'Thermodynamics', 'First law of thermodynamics', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 7 (Thermodynamics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 5', '100 J of work is done on a gas, and at the same time it gives out 40 J of heat. The change in its internal energy is', '["−60 J", "+60 J", "+140 J", "−140 J"]', 1, 'ΔU = heat added + work done on the gas = (−40) + (+100) = +60 J.', '{"0": "−60 J gets both signs wrong.", "2": "+140 J treats the heat as absorbed rather than given out.", "3": "−140 J treats the work as done by the gas and the heat as lost."}'),
  ('PHY-7207', 'Thermodynamics', 'Molar specific heats', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 7 (Thermodynamics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 5', 'The same quantity of heat is given to one mole of a diatomic ideal gas, once at constant volume (rise ΔT₁) and once at constant pressure (rise ΔT₂). The ratio ΔT₁/ΔT₂ is', '["0.71", "1.0", "1.4", "1.67"]', 2, 'Q = CvΔT₁ = CpΔT₂, so ΔT₁/ΔT₂ = Cp/Cv = γ = 7/5 = 1.4. At constant volume none of the heat does work, so the temperature rises more.', '{"0": "0.71 inverts the ratio.", "1": "The rises differ because at constant pressure some heat becomes work.", "3": "1.67 is γ for a monatomic gas."}'),
  ('PHY-7208', 'Thermodynamics', 'Heat and specific heat', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 7 (Thermodynamics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 5', 'A 2.0 kW kettle that is 80% efficient heats 1.5 kg of water from 20 °C to 100 °C. How long does it take? (specific heat of water 4200 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹)', '["252 s", "394 s", "504 s", "315 s"]', 3, 'Heat needed = 1.5 × 4200 × 80 = 504 000 J. Useful power = 0.8 × 2000 = 1600 W. t = 504 000/1600 = 315 s.', '{"0": "252 s ignores the 80% efficiency.", "1": "394 s divides by 0.8 twice.", "2": "504 s uses 1000 W of useful power."}'),
  ('PHY-7209', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric field of two charges', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 8 (Electrostatics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 6', 'Charges of +4 µC and +1 µC are 30 cm apart. The electric field is zero at a point on the line joining them', '["20 cm from the +4 µC charge, between them", "10 cm from the +4 µC charge, between them", "midway between them", "30 cm beyond the +1 µC charge"]', 0, 'Between like charges the fields oppose. k(4)/x² = k(1)/(0.30 − x)² gives x/(0.30 − x) = 2, so x = 0.20 m from the +4 µC charge (closer to the smaller charge).', '{"1": "At 10 cm from the +4 µC charge its field is 16 times that of the +1 µC charge.", "2": "At the midpoint the larger charge''s field is four times stronger.", "3": "Beyond the +1 µC charge both fields point the same way and cannot cancel."}'),
  ('PHY-7210', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric field of two charges', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 8 (Electrostatics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 6', 'Charges of +4 µC and −1 µC are 30 cm apart. The electric field is zero at a point on the line through them', '["between the charges, 20 cm from the +4 µC charge", "30 cm beyond the −1 µC charge, on the side away from the +4 µC charge", "between the charges, 10 cm from the −1 µC charge", "30 cm beyond the +4 µC charge, on the side away from the −1 µC charge"]', 1, 'Between unlike charges both fields point the same way, so the zero must lie outside, nearer the smaller charge. 4/(d + 0.30)² = 1/d² gives d + 0.30 = 2d, so d = 0.30 m beyond the −1 µC charge.', '{"0": "Between opposite charges the two fields add, so they cannot cancel there.", "2": "Between opposite charges the two fields add, so they cannot cancel there.", "3": "Beyond the larger charge its field always dominates."}'),
  ('PHY-7211', 'Electrostatics', 'Coulomb''s law in a medium', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 8 (Electrostatics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 6', 'Two point charges repel with a force of 9 N in air. They are placed in a medium of relative permittivity 3 and their separation is halved. The force becomes', '["3 N", "27 N", "12 N", "6.75 N"]', 2, 'F ∝ 1/(εr r²). The medium divides the force by 3; halving r multiplies it by 4. F = 9 × 4/3 = 12 N.', '{"0": "3 N accounts for the medium but not the closer distance.", "1": "27 N multiplies by 3 for the medium instead of dividing.", "3": "6.75 N inverts both effects (×3 for the medium, ÷4 for the distance)."}'),
  ('PHY-7212', 'Electrostatics', 'Coulomb''s law', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 8 (Electrostatics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 6', 'Three equal charges +q sit at the corners of an equilateral triangle of side a. The magnitude of the net electric force on any one of them is', '["2kq²/a²", "kq²/a²", "zero", "√3 kq²/a²"]', 3, 'Each of the other two charges pushes with F = kq²/a², and the two forces are 60° apart. Resultant = 2F cos 30° = √3 F.', '{"0": "2kq²/a² adds the forces as if they were parallel.", "1": "kq²/a² is just one of the two forces.", "2": "The forces do not cancel; they are 60° apart, not opposite."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-7213', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric field of point charges', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 8 (Electrostatics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 6', 'Charges +q, +q, −q, −q are placed at the corners of a square of side a, the two positive charges at adjacent corners. The electric field at the centre of the square has magnitude', '["4√2 kq/a², pointing towards the side with the negative charges", "zero", "2√2 kq/a², pointing towards the positive charges", "4kq/a², pointing along a diagonal"]', 0, 'Each corner is a/√2 from the centre, so each charge gives E₀ = kq/(a²/2) = 2kq/a². A positive charge and the opposite negative charge give fields in the same direction: 2E₀ along each diagonal. The two diagonal fields are perpendicular: 2E₀√2 = 4√2 kq/a², directed from the positive side to the negative side.', '{"1": "The field would be zero only if all four charges were equal.", "2": "The field points away from positive charges and towards negative ones, and its size is twice this.", "3": "4kq/a² is the field along one diagonal only."}'),
  ('PHY-7214', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric field and potential', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 8 (Electrostatics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 6', 'At the midpoint between two equal positive point charges,', '["the potential is zero but the electric field is not", "the electric field is zero but the potential is not", "both the field and the potential are zero", "neither the field nor the potential is zero"]', 1, 'The fields (vectors) of the two charges are equal and opposite at the midpoint, so E = 0. Potentials (scalars) add: V = 2kq/(d/2) ≠ 0.', '{"0": "That describes the midpoint between equal and opposite charges.", "2": "Potential is a scalar, so the two positive potentials add instead of cancelling.", "3": "The fields do cancel by symmetry."}'),
  ('PHY-7215', 'Electrostatics', 'Field due to charged sheets', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 8 (Electrostatics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 6', 'Two large parallel plates both carry the same positive surface charge density σ. The electric field in the region between them is', '["σ/ε₀", "σ/2ε₀", "zero", "2σ/ε₀"]', 2, 'Each sheet gives a field σ/2ε₀ pointing away from it on both sides. Between the plates these two fields are opposite and cancel; outside they add to σ/ε₀. (With +σ and −σ it is the other way round.)', '{"0": "σ/ε₀ is the field between plates with opposite charges, or outside these plates.", "1": "σ/2ε₀ is the field of one sheet alone.", "3": "2σ/ε₀ double-counts."}'),
  ('PHY-7216', 'Electrostatics', 'Field due to charged sheets', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 8 (Electrostatics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 6', 'A small charge placed 1 cm from a very large, uniformly charged flat sheet feels a force F. Moved to 2 cm from the sheet, it feels a force of', '["F/2", "F/4", "2F", "F"]', 3, 'The field of an infinite sheet, σ/2ε₀, does not depend on distance (as long as the sheet is very large compared with the distance). So the force stays F.', '{"0": "F/2 assumes the field ∝ 1/r, as for a long line of charge.", "1": "F/4 assumes the field ∝ 1/r², as for a point charge.", "2": "Moving away cannot increase the force."}'),
  ('PHY-7217', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric potential energy', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 8 (Electrostatics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 6', 'How much work is needed to bring a +2 µC charge from infinity to a point 30 cm from a fixed +5 µC charge?', '["0.30 J", "1.0 J", "0.030 J", "0.15 J"]', 0, 'W = kq₁q₂/r = 9 × 10⁹ × 5 × 10⁻⁶ × 2 × 10⁻⁶/0.30 = 0.30 J.', '{"1": "1.0 J divides by r² (0.09 m²), the form used for force, not for energy.", "2": "0.030 J slips a power of ten.", "3": "0.15 J includes a factor ½ that does not belong in kq₁q₂/r."}'),
  ('PHY-7218', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric field and potential', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 8 (Electrostatics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 6', 'Charges +q, −q, +q, −q are placed in that order round the corners of a square. At the centre of the square,', '["the field is zero but the potential is not", "both the electric field and the potential are zero", "the potential is zero but the field is not", "neither is zero"]', 1, 'Opposite corners hold like charges, so their fields cancel in pairs: E = 0. The potentials of two + and two − charges at equal distances cancel too: V = 0.', '{"0": "The potential also cancels, since there are equal numbers of + and − charges at the same distance.", "2": "The field also cancels, since each diagonal has two equal like charges.", "3": "Both quantities cancel by symmetry."}'),
  ('PHY-7219', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric potential', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 8 (Electrostatics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 6', 'An electron starts from rest and is accelerated through a potential difference of 100 V. Its final speed is (e = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C, m = 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ kg)', '["4.2 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹", "3.5 × 10¹³ m s⁻¹", "5.9 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹", "1.9 × 10⁷ m s⁻¹"]', 2, 'eV = ½mv², so v = √(2eV/m) = √(2 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 100/9.1 × 10⁻³¹) ≈ 5.9 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹.', '{"0": "4.2 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹ forgets the 2 in 2eV/m.", "1": "3.5 × 10¹³ m s⁻¹ is v², not v.", "3": "1.9 × 10⁷ m s⁻¹ uses 1000 V."}'),
  ('PHY-7220', 'Electrostatics', 'Potential difference in a uniform field', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 8 (Electrostatics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 6', 'A uniform electric field of 2000 V m⁻¹ points in the +x direction. Point B is 3 cm further along +x than point A. The potential difference V_A − V_B is', '["−60 V", "0 V", "+6000 V", "+60 V"]', 3, 'Potential falls in the direction of the field. ΔV = E × d = 2000 × 0.03 = 60 V, with A at the higher potential, so V_A − V_B = +60 V.', '{"0": "−60 V has the sign the wrong way round: B is downstream along the field.", "1": "Points separated along the field are at different potentials.", "2": "+6000 V uses 3 m instead of 3 cm."}'),
  ('PHY-7221', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric potential due to a point charge', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 8 (Electrostatics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 6', 'At a point near an isolated point charge, the potential is 600 V and the field strength is 200 V m⁻¹. The distance to the charge and its size are', '["3 m and 0.2 µC", "3 m and 2 µC", "0.33 m and 0.2 µC", "9 m and 0.6 µC"]', 0, 'V = kq/r and E = kq/r², so r = V/E = 600/200 = 3 m. Then q = Vr/k = 600 × 3/(9 × 10⁹) = 2 × 10⁻⁷ C = 0.2 µC.', '{"1": "2 µC is ten times too large.", "2": "0.33 m inverts V/E.", "3": "9 m squares the ratio."}'),
  ('PHY-7222', 'Electrostatics', 'Capacitance of a parallel-plate capacitor', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 8 (Electrostatics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 6', 'An air-filled parallel-plate capacitor has capacitance 10 µF. Its plate separation is doubled and the gap is filled with a dielectric of relative permittivity 4. Its capacitance becomes', '["80 µF", "20 µF", "5 µF", "40 µF"]', 1, 'C = εr ε₀A/d. ×4 for the dielectric and ÷2 for the separation: 10 × 4/2 = 20 µF.', '{"0": "80 µF multiplies by 2 for the separation instead of dividing.", "2": "5 µF ignores the dielectric.", "3": "40 µF ignores the change in separation."}'),
  ('PHY-7223', 'Electrostatics', 'Energy stored in a capacitor', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 8 (Electrostatics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 6', 'A 4 µF capacitor is charged to 200 V and then disconnected from the supply. Its plate separation is then doubled. The energy stored becomes', '["0.04 J", "0.08 J", "0.16 J", "0.32 J"]', 2, 'Initially U = ½CV² = ½ × 4 × 10⁻⁶ × 200² = 0.08 J. Disconnected, Q stays fixed; C halves, so U = Q²/2C doubles to 0.16 J. The extra energy comes from the work done pulling the plates apart.', '{"0": "0.04 J would be right if the capacitor stayed connected to the battery.", "1": "0.08 J is the energy before the plates were moved.", "3": "0.32 J quadruples instead of doubling."}'),
  ('PHY-7224', 'Electrostatics', 'Energy stored in a capacitor', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 8 (Electrostatics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 6', 'A 4 µF capacitor stays connected to a 200 V supply while its plate separation is doubled. The energy stored becomes', '["0.16 J", "0.08 J", "0.02 J", "0.04 J"]', 3, 'Connected, V stays 200 V. C halves to 2 µF, so U = ½CV² = ½ × 2 × 10⁻⁶ × 40 000 = 0.04 J (half the original 0.08 J). Charge flows back into the battery.', '{"0": "0.16 J is the result if the capacitor were disconnected (constant charge).", "1": "0.08 J is the original energy.", "2": "0.02 J quarters the energy."}'),
  ('PHY-7225', 'Electrostatics', 'Combinations of capacitors', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 8 (Electrostatics)', 'Two 6 µF capacitors are joined in series, and this pair is connected in parallel with a third 6 µF capacitor. The total capacitance is', '["9 µF", "4 µF", "18 µF", "2 µF"]', 0, 'Series pair: 6 × 6/(6 + 6) = 3 µF. In parallel with 6 µF: 3 + 6 = 9 µF.', '{"1": "4 µF puts two capacitors in parallel (12 µF) and that pair in series with the third.", "2": "18 µF puts all three in parallel.", "3": "2 µF puts all three in series."}'),
  ('PHY-7226', 'Electrostatics', 'Combinations of capacitors', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 8 (Electrostatics)', 'A 2 µF capacitor charged to 100 V is connected across an uncharged 3 µF capacitor. The common potential difference and the energy lost are', '["40 V; no energy lost", "40 V; 6 mJ lost", "60 V; 4 mJ lost", "50 V; 5 mJ lost"]', 1, 'Charge is shared: Q = 2 × 10⁻⁶ × 100 = 200 µC; V = 200 µC/5 µF = 40 V. Energy before ½ × 2 µF × 100² = 10 mJ; after ½ × 5 µF × 40² = 4 mJ. 6 mJ is lost as heat and radiation in the connecting wires.', '{"0": "Charge is conserved but energy is not: some is always lost when capacitors share charge.", "2": "60 V shares the voltage in proportion to capacitance (3/5 of 100 V), and 4 mJ is the energy left, not the energy lost.", "3": "50 V assumes equal capacitances."}'),
  ('PHY-7227', 'Electrostatics', 'Charging and discharging a capacitor', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 8 (Electrostatics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 6', 'A 5 µF capacitor discharges through a 2 MΩ resistor. The time for the voltage across it to fall to half its initial value is', '["10 s", "5 s", "6.9 s", "14 s"]', 2, 'Time constant τ = RC = 2 × 10⁶ × 5 × 10⁻⁶ = 10 s. V = V₀e^(−t/τ), so half-time t = τ ln 2 = 6.9 s.', '{"0": "10 s is one time constant, when V has fallen to 37%, not 50%.", "1": "5 s is τ/2, which is not how exponential decay works.", "3": "14 s is τ/ln 2."}'),
  ('PHY-7228', 'Electrostatics', 'Charging and discharging a capacitor', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 8 (Electrostatics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 6', 'A capacitor is charged through a resistor from a battery. At time t = 2RC after switching on, the current is what percentage of its initial value?', '["86.5%", "37%", "25%", "13.5%"]', 3, 'The charging current decays as I = I₀e^(−t/RC). At t = 2RC, I/I₀ = e⁻² ≈ 0.135. (The charge has reached 86.5% of its final value.)', '{"0": "86.5% is the charge, not the current, at 2RC.", "1": "37% is the current after one time constant.", "2": "25% assumes the current halves every RC."}'),
  ('PHY-7229', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric field between parallel plates', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 8 (Electrostatics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 6', 'An oil drop of mass 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁵ kg is held stationary between horizontal plates 2.0 cm apart with 500 V across them. The charge on the drop is (g = 10 m s⁻², e = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C)', '["6.4 × 10⁻¹⁹ C (four electron charges)", "3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ C (two electron charges)", "1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C (one electron charge)", "6.4 × 10⁻¹⁷ C (400 electron charges)"]', 0, 'E = V/d = 500/0.020 = 2.5 × 10⁴ V m⁻¹. Balance: qE = mg, so q = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁴/2.5 × 10⁴ = 6.4 × 10⁻¹⁹ C = 4e.', '{"1": "3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ C uses d = 1 cm.", "2": "1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C uses d = 0.5 cm.", "3": "6.4 × 10⁻¹⁷ C uses d = 2.0 m instead of 2.0 cm."}'),
  ('PHY-7230', 'Electrostatics', 'Electric potential energy', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 8 (Electrostatics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 6', 'Three equal charges q are placed at the corners of an equilateral triangle of side a. The electric potential energy of the arrangement is', '["kq²/a", "3kq²/a", "√3 kq²/a", "zero"]', 1, 'Potential energy is a sum over pairs. Three pairs, each with energy kq²/a: U = 3kq²/a. This equals the work needed to assemble the charges from infinity.', '{"0": "kq²/a counts only one pair.", "2": "√3 kq²/a mixes in the vector addition used for forces; energy is a scalar.", "3": "Like charges have positive potential energy; it cannot cancel."}'),
  ('PHY-7231', 'Current electricity', 'Resistivity', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 9 (Current electricity); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 7', 'A wire of resistance R is stretched uniformly to three times its original length. Its new resistance is', '["3R", "R/3", "9R", "6R"]', 2, 'Volume is constant, so tripling the length divides the area by 3. R = ρL/A becomes ρ(3L)/(A/3) = 9R.', '{"0": "3R forgets that the wire also gets thinner.", "1": "R/3 has the length effect upside down.", "3": "6R adds the two factors instead of multiplying them."}'),
  ('PHY-7232', 'Current electricity', 'Combinations of resistors', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 9 (Current electricity); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 7', 'A uniform wire of resistance 16 Ω is bent into a circle. The resistance between two diametrically opposite points on it is', '["8 Ω", "16 Ω", "2 Ω", "4 Ω"]', 3, 'The two halves (8 Ω each) are in parallel between the points: 8 × 8/16 = 4 Ω.', '{"0": "8 Ω is one half on its own.", "1": "16 Ω is the whole wire in series.", "2": "2 Ω divides the half-ring resistance by 4 instead of 2."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-7233', 'Current electricity', 'Temperature coefficient of resistance', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 9 (Current electricity); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 7', 'A copper wire has a resistance of 20 Ω at 20 °C. Taking α = 0.004 K⁻¹, its resistance at 70 °C is', '["24 Ω", "21.6 Ω", "28 Ω", "25.6 Ω"]', 0, 'R = R₀(1 + αΔT) = 20(1 + 0.004 × 50) = 20 × 1.2 = 24 Ω.', '{"1": "21.6 Ω uses ΔT = 20 K, the starting temperature.", "2": "28 Ω uses ΔT = 100 K.", "3": "25.6 Ω uses ΔT = 70 K, the final temperature rather than the change."}'),
  ('PHY-7234', 'Current electricity', 'Temperature coefficient of resistance', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 9 (Current electricity); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 7', 'A platinum resistance thermometer reads 10.00 Ω at 0 °C and 13.90 Ω at 100 °C. In a furnace it reads 12.34 Ω. Assuming a linear change, the furnace temperature is', '["50 °C", "60 °C", "23.4 °C", "88.8 °C"]', 1, 'Resistance rises 3.90 Ω per 100 °C. The rise in the furnace is 2.34 Ω, so θ = 2.34/3.90 × 100 = 60 °C.', '{"0": "50 °C assumes the reading is half-way, which it is not.", "2": "23.4 °C uses 2.34 × 10.", "3": "88.8 °C divides 12.34 by 13.90."}'),
  ('PHY-7235', 'Current electricity', 'Internal resistance', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 9 (Current electricity); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 7', 'A cell of emf 12 V and internal resistance 0.5 Ω is connected to a 5.5 Ω resistor. The terminal potential difference is', '["12 V", "1 V", "11 V", "10 V"]', 2, 'I = E/(R + r) = 12/6 = 2 A. Terminal p.d. V = E − Ir = 12 − 1 = 11 V (= IR = 2 × 5.5).', '{"0": "12 V is the emf, the terminal p.d. only when no current flows.", "1": "1 V is the \"lost volts\" across the internal resistance.", "3": "10 V doubles the lost volts."}'),
  ('PHY-7236', 'Current electricity', 'Internal resistance', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 9 (Current electricity); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 7', 'A battery''s terminal p.d. is 1.4 V when it supplies 0.2 A and 1.2 V when it supplies 0.6 A. Its emf and internal resistance are', '["1.6 V and 1.0 Ω", "1.5 V and 1.0 Ω", "1.4 V and 0.5 Ω", "1.5 V and 0.5 Ω"]', 3, 'V = E − Ir. Subtracting: 0.2 = 0.4r, so r = 0.5 Ω. Then E = 1.4 + 0.2 × 0.5 = 1.5 V.', '{"0": "1.6 V and 1.0 Ω uses r = 0.2/0.2.", "1": "1.0 Ω does not fit both readings.", "2": "1.4 V is a terminal p.d. under load, which is less than the emf."}'),
  ('PHY-7237', 'Current electricity', 'Maximum power transfer', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 9 (Current electricity); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 7', 'A battery of emf 6 V and internal resistance 2 Ω is connected to a variable load. The greatest power the load can receive is', '["4.5 W", "9 W", "18 W", "4 W"]', 0, 'Maximum power is delivered when R = r = 2 Ω: I = 6/4 = 1.5 A, P = I²R = 2.25 × 2 = 4.5 W (= E²/4r). The efficiency is then only 50%.', '{"1": "9 W is E²/2r, double the true maximum.", "2": "18 W is E²/r, the power if the battery were short-circuited, all wasted inside it.", "3": "4 W is the power in a 4 Ω load, which is less than the maximum."}'),
  ('PHY-7238', 'Current electricity', 'Maximum power transfer', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 9 (Current electricity); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 7', 'The battery (emf 6 V, internal resistance 2 Ω) drives a 4 Ω load. The power in the load and the efficiency are', '["4.5 W and 50%", "4 W and 67%", "4 W and 33%", "6 W and 67%"]', 1, 'I = 6/6 = 1 A. Load power = I²R = 4 W. Total power EI = 6 W, so efficiency = 4/6 ≈ 67%. A load larger than r receives less than the maximum (4.5 W) but at higher efficiency.', '{"0": "4.5 W and 50% are the values for a 2 Ω load (maximum power).", "2": "33% is the fraction wasted inside the battery.", "3": "6 W is the total power from the battery, not the load power."}'),
  ('PHY-7239', 'Current electricity', 'Combinations of resistors', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 9 (Current electricity); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 7', 'Two 6 Ω resistors in parallel are connected in series with a third 6 Ω resistor across a 9 V supply of negligible internal resistance. The current in each of the parallel resistors is', '["1.0 A", "1.5 A", "0.5 A", "0.75 A"]', 2, 'Parallel pair = 3 Ω; total 9 Ω; I = 1 A. It splits equally between the two equal parallel resistors: 0.5 A each.', '{"0": "1.0 A is the total current.", "1": "1.5 A is 9 V/6 Ω, ignoring the other resistors.", "3": "0.75 A splits the 1.5 A that would flow through a single 6 Ω resistor."}'),
  ('PHY-7240', 'Current electricity', 'Electric power', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 9 (Current electricity); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 7', 'A 100 W bulb and a 25 W bulb (both rated for 220 V) are connected in series across 220 V. Which glows brighter?', '["The 100 W bulb, because it has the larger power rating", "Both glow equally, since the same current flows", "Neither glows, because they are in series", "The 25 W bulb, because it has the larger resistance"]', 3, 'R = V²/P: the 25 W bulb has 1936 Ω, the 100 W bulb 484 Ω. In series the current is the same, so power I²R is larger in the higher resistance: the 25 W bulb is brighter.', '{"0": "The rating applies only at 220 V each; in series the current is shared and I²R decides.", "1": "Same current, but different resistances, so different powers.", "2": "Current flows through both bulbs in series."}'),
  ('PHY-7241', 'Current electricity', 'Electric power', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 9 (Current electricity); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 7', 'A 100 W and a 25 W bulb, both rated at 220 V, are connected in series across a 220 V supply. Assuming their resistances do not change, the total power drawn is', '["20 W", "125 W", "75 W", "62.5 W"]', 0, 'R₁ = 220²/100 = 484 Ω, R₂ = 220²/25 = 1936 Ω. Total 2420 Ω. P = 220²/2420 = 20 W, less than either rating.', '{"1": "125 W adds the ratings, which applies only in parallel.", "2": "75 W subtracts the ratings.", "3": "62.5 W averages them."}'),
  ('PHY-7242', 'Current electricity', 'Steady current', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 9 (Current electricity); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 7', 'A steady current of 4.8 A flows in a wire. How many electrons pass any cross-section each second? (e = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C)', '["4.8 × 10¹⁹", "3.0 × 10¹⁹", "7.7 × 10⁻¹⁹", "3.0 × 10¹⁸"]', 1, 'n = I/e = 4.8/1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 3.0 × 10¹⁹ electrons per second.', '{"0": "4.8 × 10¹⁹ uses e = 10⁻¹⁹ C.", "2": "7.7 × 10⁻¹⁹ multiplies I by e.", "3": "3.0 × 10¹⁸ slips a power of ten."}'),
  ('PHY-7243', 'Current electricity', 'Ohm''s law', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 9 (Current electricity); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 7', 'Resistors of 4 kΩ and 8 kΩ are in series across a 12 V supply. A voltmeter of resistance 8 kΩ is connected across the 8 kΩ resistor. It reads', '["8 V", "4 V", "6 V", "12 V"]', 2, 'The voltmeter in parallel with 8 kΩ gives 4 kΩ. The circuit is now 4 kΩ + 4 kΩ, so the reading is 6 V, not the 8 V that would be there without the meter.', '{"0": "8 V is the p.d. before the voltmeter is connected; a meter of modest resistance loads the circuit.", "1": "4 V is the p.d. across the 4 kΩ resistor before the meter is added.", "3": "12 V is the supply voltage."}'),
  ('PHY-7244', 'Current electricity', 'Kirchhoff''s rules', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 9 (Current electricity)', 'A 6 V cell in series with 1 Ω and a 3 V cell in series with 2 Ω are connected in parallel (positive terminals together) across a 2 Ω load. The cells have no internal resistance. The p.d. across the load is', '["4.5 V", "9 V", "3 V", "3.75 V"]', 3, 'Let the load p.d. be V. Currents into the top node: (6 − V)/1 + (3 − V)/2 = V/2. So 7.5 = 2V and V = 3.75 V. Note the 3 V cell is being charged (its branch current is negative).', '{"0": "4.5 V is the average of the emfs.", "1": "9 V adds the emfs as if in series.", "2": "3 V is the emf of the smaller cell alone."}'),
  ('PHY-7245', 'Current electricity', 'Wheatstone bridge', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 9 (Current electricity)', 'A Wheatstone bridge is balanced. If the battery and the galvanometer are then interchanged,', '["the bridge stays balanced", "the galvanometer deflects fully", "the bridge becomes balanced only if all four resistors are equal", "current through the galvanometer doubles"]', 0, 'Balance needs P/Q = R/S. Swapping the battery and galvanometer gives the condition P/R = Q/S, which is the same equation rearranged, so the bridge is still balanced.', '{"1": "The balance condition is unchanged, so no current flows through the galvanometer.", "2": "Balance needs only the ratio condition, not equal resistors.", "3": "At balance the galvanometer current is zero both before and after."}'),
  ('PHY-7246', 'Current electricity', 'Ohm''s law', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 9 (Current electricity); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 7', 'The current-voltage graph of a filament lamp bends towards the voltage axis at higher voltages. This is because', '["the filament gets hotter, so its resistance decreases", "the filament gets hotter, so its resistance increases", "the lamp obeys Ohm''s law exactly", "the voltage source has internal resistance"]', 1, 'Metal resistance rises with temperature. As the voltage increases, each extra volt drives less extra current, so the I-V graph curves towards the V axis. The lamp is non-ohmic.', '{"0": "Falling resistance would make the graph bend towards the current axis (as for a thermistor).", "2": "An ohmic conductor gives a straight line through the origin.", "3": "The graph is of the lamp''s own V and I; the source does not change its shape."}'),
  ('PHY-7247', 'Current electricity', 'Resistivity', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 9 (Current electricity); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 7', 'A potential difference V drives a current I through a wire. A second wire of the same material has twice the diameter and half the length. The same V drives through it a current of', '["2I", "4I", "8I", "I/8"]', 2, 'R ∝ L/d². Half the length and twice the diameter: R′ = R × ½ ÷ 4 = R/8. With the same V, I′ = 8I.', '{"0": "2I accounts only for the length.", "1": "4I accounts only for the diameter.", "3": "I/8 inverts the effect: the second wire has the smaller resistance."}'),
  ('PHY-7248', 'Current electricity', 'Electric power', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 9 (Current electricity); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 7', 'An electric motor draws 5 A from a 220 V supply and is 75% efficient. Its mechanical output power is', '["1100 W", "275 W", "1467 W", "825 W"]', 3, 'Input power = VI = 220 × 5 = 1100 W. Output = 0.75 × 1100 = 825 W.', '{"0": "1100 W is the input power.", "1": "275 W is the power wasted (25%).", "2": "1467 W divides by 0.75 instead of multiplying."}'),
  ('PHY-7249', 'Electromagnetism', 'Charged particle in a magnetic field', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 10 (Electromagnetism); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 8', 'A proton (m = 1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ kg, q = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C) moves at 2.0 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹ at right angles to a uniform field of 0.50 T. The radius of its circular path is about', '["4.2 cm", "2.1 cm", "8.4 cm", "0.42 cm"]', 0, 'r = mv/qB = (1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ × 2.0 × 10⁶)/(1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 0.50) ≈ 0.042 m.', '{"1": "2.1 cm uses B = 1.0 T.", "2": "8.4 cm uses B = 0.25 T.", "3": "0.42 cm slips a power of ten."}'),
  ('PHY-7250', 'Electromagnetism', 'Charged particle in a magnetic field', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 10 (Electromagnetism); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 8', 'A proton and an alpha particle enter the same uniform magnetic field at right angles to it with equal kinetic energies. The ratio of the radii of their paths r_p : r_α is', '["1 : 2", "1 : 1", "2 : 1", "1 : √2"]', 1, 'r = mv/qB = √(2mK)/qB. For the alpha particle, m is 4 times and q is 2 times that of the proton: √4/2 = 1. So the radii are equal.', '{"0": "1 : 2 is the ratio for equal speeds, not equal kinetic energies.", "2": "2 : 1 inverts the equal-speed ratio.", "3": "1 : √2 uses √m but forgets the charge."}'),
  ('PHY-7251', 'Electromagnetism', 'Charged particle in a magnetic field', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 10 (Electromagnetism); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 8', 'A proton and an alpha particle enter the same uniform magnetic field at right angles to it with equal speeds. The ratio of the radii of their paths r_p : r_α is', '["1 : 1", "2 : 1", "1 : 2", "1 : 4"]', 2, 'r = mv/qB ∝ m/q for equal speeds. Proton m/q = 1 unit; alpha m/q = 4/2 = 2 units. So r_p : r_α = 1 : 2.', '{"0": "1 : 1 is the ratio for equal kinetic energies.", "1": "2 : 1 inverts the ratio.", "3": "1 : 4 uses the masses but forgets the alpha particle''s double charge."}'),
  ('PHY-7252', 'Electromagnetism', 'Charged particle in a magnetic field', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 10 (Electromagnetism); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 8', 'An electron moves in a circle at right angles to a uniform magnetic field. If its speed is doubled, the time for one revolution', '["is halved", "is doubled", "is quartered", "stays the same"]', 3, 'T = 2πr/v = 2πm/qB. Doubling v doubles r as well, so the period does not depend on speed. (This is why a cyclotron can use a fixed frequency.)', '{"0": "Halving would be true only if the radius stayed fixed.", "1": "A faster particle does not take longer; its larger circle exactly compensates.", "2": "The period has no 1/v² dependence."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-7253', 'Electromagnetism', 'Magnetic flux', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 10 (Electromagnetism); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 8', 'A flat coil of area 0.020 m² is in a uniform field of 0.50 T that makes an angle of 60° with the plane of the coil. The magnetic flux through the coil is', '["8.7 × 10⁻³ Wb", "5.0 × 10⁻³ Wb", "1.0 × 10⁻² Wb", "0.87 Wb"]', 0, 'Φ = BA cosθ with θ measured from the normal. A field at 60° to the plane is at 30° to the normal: Φ = 0.50 × 0.020 × cos 30° ≈ 8.7 × 10⁻³ Wb.', '{"1": "5.0 × 10⁻³ Wb uses cos 60°, taking the angle to the plane as if it were to the normal.", "2": "1.0 × 10⁻² Wb assumes the field is along the normal.", "3": "0.87 Wb forgets the area."}'),
  ('PHY-7254', 'Electromagnetism', 'Force on a current-carrying conductor', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 10 (Electromagnetism); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 8', 'A 0.50 m wire carrying 4.0 A lies at 30° to a uniform magnetic field of 0.20 T. The force on it is', '["0.35 N", "0.20 N", "0.40 N", "0.10 N"]', 1, 'F = BIL sinθ = 0.20 × 4.0 × 0.50 × sin 30° = 0.20 N.', '{"0": "0.35 N uses cos 30°.", "2": "0.40 N assumes the wire is perpendicular to the field.", "3": "0.10 N halves again."}'),
  ('PHY-7255', 'Electromagnetism', 'Force on a moving charge', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 10 (Electromagnetism); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 8', 'A charged particle moves in a circle at right angles to a uniform magnetic field. Which quantity stays constant?', '["Its velocity", "Its momentum", "Its kinetic energy", "The magnetic force vector on it"]', 2, 'The magnetic force is always perpendicular to the velocity, so it does no work: speed and kinetic energy stay constant. The direction of motion, and with it the velocity, momentum and force vectors, keep changing.', '{"0": "Velocity is a vector whose direction keeps changing.", "1": "Momentum is a vector whose direction keeps changing.", "3": "The force keeps the same size but always points to the centre, so its direction changes."}'),
  ('PHY-7256', 'Electromagnetism', 'Crossed electric and magnetic fields', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 10 (Electromagnetism); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 8', 'An electric field of 3.0 × 10⁴ V m⁻¹ and a magnetic field of 0.20 T are at right angles to each other and to a beam of charged particles. Particles pass straight through, undeflected, if their speed is', '["6.0 × 10³ m s⁻¹", "6.0 × 10⁵ m s⁻¹", "1.5 × 10³ m s⁻¹", "1.5 × 10⁵ m s⁻¹"]', 3, 'No deflection when qE = qvB, so v = E/B = 3.0 × 10⁴/0.20 = 1.5 × 10⁵ m s⁻¹, whatever the charge or mass.', '{"0": "6.0 × 10³ m s⁻¹ multiplies E by B.", "1": "6.0 × 10⁵ m s⁻¹ is four times too large.", "2": "1.5 × 10³ m s⁻¹ slips two powers of ten."}'),
  ('PHY-7257', 'Electromagnetism', 'Charged particle in a magnetic field', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 10 (Electromagnetism); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 8', 'An electron is accelerated from rest through 200 V and then enters a uniform magnetic field of 1.0 mT at right angles. Taking e/m = 1.76 × 10¹¹ C kg⁻¹, the radius of its path is about', '["4.8 cm", "2.4 cm", "9.5 cm", "0.48 cm"]', 0, 'v = √(2(e/m)V) = √(2 × 1.76 × 10¹¹ × 200) ≈ 8.4 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹. r = v/((e/m)B) = 8.4 × 10⁶/(1.76 × 10⁸) ≈ 0.048 m.', '{"1": "2.4 cm uses B = 2.0 mT.", "2": "9.5 cm would need an accelerating voltage of 800 V.", "3": "0.48 cm slips a power of ten."}'),
  ('PHY-7258', 'Electromagnetism', 'Charged particle in a magnetic field', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 10 (Electromagnetism); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 8', 'A proton enters a uniform magnetic field with its velocity at 30° to the field lines. Its path is', '["a circle", "a helix around the field lines", "a straight line", "a parabola"]', 1, 'The component of velocity along B is unaffected, while the perpendicular component makes the proton circle. Together they give a helix.', '{"0": "A circle happens only when the velocity is exactly perpendicular to B.", "2": "A straight line happens only when the velocity is parallel to B.", "3": "A parabola is the path in a uniform electric field."}'),
  ('PHY-7259', 'Electromagnetism', 'Magnetic flux density', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 10 (Electromagnetism); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 8', 'The tesla, the SI unit of magnetic flux density, is equivalent to', '["kg s⁻¹ C", "N m A⁻¹", "kg s⁻² A⁻¹", "Wb m"]', 2, 'From F = BIL, B = F/IL, so 1 T = 1 N A⁻¹ m⁻¹ = (kg m s⁻²)/(A m) = kg s⁻² A⁻¹. It is also 1 Wb m⁻².', '{"0": "kg s⁻¹ C⁻¹ would be right (since A = C s⁻¹); kg s⁻¹ C has the charge on top.", "1": "N m A⁻¹ multiplies by length instead of dividing.", "3": "Flux density is flux per area, Wb m⁻², not Wb m."}'),
  ('PHY-7260', 'Electromagnetism', 'Magnetic flux', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 10 (Electromagnetism); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 8', 'A flat coil of area A has its normal along a uniform magnetic field B. It is rotated through 180°. The magnitude of the change in flux through it is', '["zero", "BA", "BA/2", "2BA"]', 3, 'The flux changes from +BA to −BA, since the field now enters through the other face. |ΔΦ| = 2BA.', '{"0": "The flux reverses, so it does not return to the same value.", "1": "BA is the change for a 90° turn.", "2": "BA/2 is the change for a 60° turn."}'),
  ('PHY-7261', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'Faraday''s law', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 11 (Electromagnetic induction); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 9', 'A 100-turn coil of area 0.050 m² lies at right angles to a magnetic field that falls steadily from 0.80 T to 0.20 T in 0.10 s. The average induced emf is', '["30 V", "0.30 V", "50 V", "40 V"]', 0, 'ε = N ΔΦ/Δt = 100 × 0.050 × (0.80 − 0.20)/0.10 = 30 V.', '{"1": "0.30 V forgets the 100 turns.", "2": "50 V uses ΔB = 1.0 T (adds the two fields).", "3": "40 V takes the field as falling from 0.80 T all the way to zero."}'),
  ('PHY-7262', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'Motional emf', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 11 (Electromagnetic induction); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 9', 'A 0.50 m metal rod slides at a steady 4.0 m s⁻¹ along rails in a uniform field of 0.30 T at right angles to the rails. The rails are joined by a 2.0 Ω resistor (other resistances negligible). The force needed to keep the rod moving is', '["0.60 N", "0.045 N", "0.30 N", "0.090 N"]', 1, 'ε = BLv = 0.30 × 0.50 × 4.0 = 0.60 V; I = 0.60/2.0 = 0.30 A. The magnetic force opposing the motion is BIL = 0.30 × 0.30 × 0.50 = 0.045 N, so an equal pull is needed.', '{"0": "0.60 is the emf in volts.", "2": "0.30 is the current in amperes.", "3": "0.090 N doubles the force."}'),
  ('PHY-7263', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'Lenz''s law and energy', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 11 (Electromagnetic induction); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 9', 'In the rod-on-rails circuit (B = 0.30 T, L = 0.50 m, v = 4.0 m s⁻¹, R = 2.0 Ω), the mechanical power supplied by whoever pulls the rod is', '["0.045 W, more than the heat produced", "0.36 W, twice the heat produced", "0.18 W, equal to the heat produced in the resistor", "0, because the rod moves at constant speed"]', 2, 'P = Fv = 0.045 × 4.0 = 0.18 W, and I²R = 0.30² × 2.0 = 0.18 W. The work done against the Lenz force becomes electrical energy and then heat: energy is conserved.', '{"0": "0.045 is the force in newtons, not the power.", "1": "The power supplied equals the heat produced; it is not twice it.", "3": "Constant speed means zero net force, but the pull still does work against the magnetic force."}'),
  ('PHY-7264', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'Lenz''s law', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 11 (Electromagnetic induction); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 9', 'The north pole of a bar magnet is pushed towards one face of a coil. Viewed from the magnet''s side, the induced current in the coil flows', '["clockwise, making that face a north pole", "anticlockwise, making that face a south pole", "clockwise, making that face a south pole", "anticlockwise, making that face a north pole"]', 3, 'Lenz: the induced current opposes the approach, so the near face becomes a north pole to repel the magnet. A current that looks anticlockwise from outside a face makes that face a north pole.', '{"0": "A clockwise current (seen from the face) makes a south pole.", "1": "An anticlockwise current makes a north pole, not a south pole.", "2": "A south pole would attract the magnet and help the motion, breaking energy conservation."}'),
  ('PHY-7265', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'Lenz''s law', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 11 (Electromagnetic induction); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 9', 'A strong magnet dropped down a vertical copper pipe falls much more slowly than when dropped down a plastic pipe. This is because', '["eddy currents induced in the copper produce a magnetic field that opposes the magnet''s motion", "copper is a magnetic material and attracts the magnet", "air resistance is larger in a copper pipe", "the copper pipe is heavier"]', 0, 'As the magnet falls, the flux through each ring of copper changes, inducing eddy currents. By Lenz''s law their fields oppose the motion, so the magnet falls slowly and the copper warms slightly.', '{"1": "Copper is not ferromagnetic; a stationary magnet is not attracted to it.", "2": "Air resistance is the same in both pipes.", "3": "The mass of the pipe does not affect the magnet''s fall."}'),
  ('PHY-7266', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'Lenz''s law', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 11 (Electromagnetic induction); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 9', 'A bar magnet is dropped vertically through a horizontal closed metal ring. Ignoring air resistance, the acceleration of the magnet', '["is less than g while approaching and greater than g while moving away", "is less than g both while it approaches the ring and while it moves away from it", "is greater than g while approaching and less than g while moving away", "is g throughout, since the ring is not magnetic"]', 1, 'Approaching, the induced current repels the magnet; leaving, it attracts the magnet back. Either way the force opposes the motion (upward), so the acceleration is less than g in both stages.', '{"0": "While leaving, the ring pulls the magnet back, which also slows it.", "2": "The induced force opposes the motion at every stage.", "3": "Induced currents flow in the ring even though it is not magnetic."}'),
  ('PHY-7267', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'AC generator', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 11 (Electromagnetic induction); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 9', 'A generator coil of 200 turns and area 0.010 m² rotates at 50 revolutions per second in a uniform field of 0.20 T. The peak emf is about', '["63 V", "20 V", "126 V", "251 V"]', 2, 'ε₀ = NBAω with ω = 2π × 50 = 314 rad s⁻¹: 200 × 0.20 × 0.010 × 314 ≈ 126 V.', '{"0": "63 V uses ω = π × 50.", "1": "20 V uses ω = 50 rad s⁻¹ (forgets the 2π).", "3": "251 V uses ω = 4π × 50."}'),
  ('PHY-7268', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'AC generator', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 11 (Electromagnetic induction); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 9', 'In a simple AC generator, the induced emf is zero at the instant when', '["the plane of the coil is parallel to the magnetic field", "the flux through the coil is zero", "the coil is rotating fastest", "the plane of the coil is perpendicular to the magnetic field"]', 3, 'emf = rate of change of flux. When the coil''s plane is perpendicular to B, the flux is at its maximum and momentarily not changing, so the emf is zero. When the plane is parallel to B, the flux is zero but changing fastest, so the emf is greatest.', '{"0": "With the plane parallel to B, the flux is zero but changing fastest: emf is maximum.", "1": "Zero flux is the moment of maximum emf, not zero emf.", "2": "The coil turns at a steady rate; the emf depends on its orientation."}'),
  ('PHY-7269', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'Transformer', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 11 (Electromagnetic induction); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 9', 'An ideal transformer steps 2200 V down to 220 V and has 5000 turns on its primary. If the secondary delivers 10 A, the number of secondary turns and the primary current are', '["500 turns and 1.0 A", "500 turns and 100 A", "50 000 turns and 1.0 A", "500 turns and 10 A"]', 0, 'Nₛ/Nₚ = Vₛ/Vₚ = 1/10, so Nₛ = 500. Power in = power out: 2200 Iₚ = 220 × 10, so Iₚ = 1.0 A.', '{"1": "A step-down transformer steps current up, so the primary current is smaller, not larger.", "2": "50 000 turns would step the voltage up.", "3": "The currents differ by the turns ratio; they are not equal."}'),
  ('PHY-7270', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'Power transmission', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 11 (Electromagnetic induction); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 9', '100 kW is sent along cables of total resistance 5 Ω. How much power is lost in the cables if it is transmitted at 10 kV?', '["50 kW", "500 W", "50 W", "1 kW"]', 1, 'I = P/V = 100 000/10 000 = 10 A. Loss = I²R = 100 × 5 = 500 W. At 1 kV the current would be 100 A and the loss 50 kW, which is why power is transmitted at high voltage.', '{"0": "50 kW is the loss at 1 kV.", "2": "50 W uses I = √10 A.", "3": "1 kW counts the 5 Ω twice, as if it were the resistance of each of two cables."}'),
  ('PHY-7271', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'Transformer', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 11 (Electromagnetic induction); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 9', 'A transformer is 90% efficient. Its primary takes 2.0 A from a 220 V supply and its secondary voltage is 22 V. The secondary current is', '["20 A", "22 A", "18 A", "1.8 A"]', 2, 'Input power = 220 × 2.0 = 440 W. Output = 0.90 × 440 = 396 W. Iₛ = 396/22 = 18 A.', '{"0": "20 A assumes 100% efficiency.", "1": "22 A divides by 0.9 instead of multiplying.", "3": "1.8 A slips a power of ten."}'),
  ('PHY-7272', 'Electromagnetic induction', 'Transformer', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 11 (Electromagnetic induction); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 9', 'The primary of a step-up transformer is connected to a steady 12 V battery. Once the switch has been closed for a while, the secondary output is', '["12 V", "greater than 12 V", "a steady voltage set by the turns ratio", "zero"]', 3, 'A transformer works only when the flux in its core changes. Steady DC gives constant flux after the switch-on moment, so no emf is induced in the secondary (there is only a brief pulse when switching on or off).', '{"0": "12 V would need the secondary to be connected directly to the battery.", "1": "Stepping up needs a changing flux, which steady DC does not give.", "2": "With DC there is no changing flux, so the turns ratio has nothing to act on."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-7273', 'Alternating current', 'RMS values', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 12 (Alternating current)', 'A 55 Ω heater is connected to a 220 V (rms) AC supply. The peak current through it is about', '["5.7 A", "4.0 A", "2.8 A", "8.0 A"]', 0, 'I_rms = 220/55 = 4.0 A. Peak current = √2 × 4.0 ≈ 5.7 A.', '{"1": "4.0 A is the rms current.", "2": "2.8 A divides by √2 instead of multiplying.", "3": "8.0 A doubles instead of multiplying by √2."}'),
  ('PHY-7274', 'Alternating current', 'Reactance', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 12 (Alternating current)', 'A 0.10 H inductor and a 100 µF capacitor are each connected in turn to the same variable-frequency AC source. At what frequency are their reactances equal?', '["316 Hz", "50 Hz", "16 Hz", "100 Hz"]', 1, 'X_L = 2πfL and X_C = 1/(2πfC). Equal when f = 1/(2π√(LC)) = 1/(2π√(10⁻⁵)) ≈ 50 Hz.', '{"0": "316 is 1/√(LC), the angular frequency in rad s⁻¹, not the frequency in Hz.", "2": "16 Hz uses LC = 10⁻⁴ s² instead of 10⁻⁵ s².", "3": "100 Hz has no basis in the formula."}'),
  ('PHY-7275', 'Alternating current', 'Phase in AC circuits', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 12 (Alternating current)', 'Which statement about phase in AC circuits is correct?', '["In a pure capacitor, the current lags the voltage by 90°", "In a pure resistor, the current leads the voltage by 90°", "In a pure inductor, the current lags the voltage by 90°", "In a pure inductor, the current leads the voltage by 90°"]', 2, 'An inductor opposes changes in current, so the current reaches its peak a quarter cycle after the voltage. In a capacitor the current leads by 90°; in a resistor they are in phase.', '{"0": "In a capacitor the current leads the voltage.", "1": "In a resistor the current and voltage are in phase.", "3": "In an inductor the current lags; it does not lead."}'),
  ('PHY-7276', 'Alternating current', 'Power in AC circuits', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 12 (Alternating current)', 'A pure capacitor is connected to an AC supply. The average power it takes over a complete cycle is', '["V_rms I_rms", "V₀I₀/2", "V₀I₀", "zero"]', 3, 'With a 90° phase difference, the capacitor takes energy from the supply for one quarter cycle and returns it in the next. The average power is zero (power factor cos 90° = 0).', '{"0": "V_rms I_rms is the average power only when V and I are in phase.", "1": "V₀I₀/2 is the same as V_rms I_rms, valid for a resistor.", "2": "V₀I₀ is the peak instantaneous power in a resistor."}'),
  ('PHY-7277', 'Alternating current', 'Reactance', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 12 (Alternating current)', 'A lamp in series with a capacitor glows on an AC supply. If the supply frequency is increased (same voltage), the lamp', '["glows more brightly, because the capacitor''s reactance falls", "glows more dimly, because the capacitor''s reactance rises", "is unaffected, since the voltage is unchanged", "goes out, because a capacitor blocks AC"]', 0, 'X_C = 1/(2πfC) falls as f rises, so more current flows and the lamp brightens. (A capacitor blocks DC, i.e. f = 0, but passes AC.)', '{"1": "Capacitive reactance falls with frequency; it is inductive reactance that rises.", "2": "The current depends on the reactance, which changes with frequency.", "3": "A capacitor passes AC; it blocks only steady DC."}'),
  ('PHY-7278', 'Alternating current', 'AC equations', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 12 (Alternating current)', 'An AC voltage is given by V = 311 sin(100πt) volts. Its frequency and rms value are', '["100 Hz and 311 V", "50 Hz and 220 V", "50 Hz and 311 V", "100π Hz and 220 V"]', 1, 'ω = 100π = 2πf, so f = 50 Hz. V_rms = 311/√2 ≈ 220 V.', '{"0": "100 Hz forgets the 2 in ω = 2πf, and 311 V is the peak value.", "2": "311 V is the peak value, not the rms value.", "3": "100π is ω in rad s⁻¹, not f in Hz."}'),
  ('PHY-7279', 'Alternating current', 'AC equations', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 12 (Alternating current)', 'A 50 Hz sinusoidal voltage starts from zero. How long does it take to first reach half of its peak value?', '["5.0 ms", "3.33 ms", "1.67 ms", "10 ms"]', 2, 'V = V₀ sin(2πft). sin(2πft) = ½ when 2πft = π/6, so t = 1/(12f) = 1/600 s ≈ 1.67 ms.', '{"0": "5.0 ms is a quarter cycle, the time to reach the full peak.", "1": "3.33 ms is T/6, which would give sin = √3/2.", "3": "10 ms is half a cycle."}'),
  ('PHY-7280', 'Alternating current', 'Electromagnetic spectrum', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 12 (Alternating current)', 'Which list puts these electromagnetic waves in order of increasing wavelength?', '["Radio waves, microwaves, infrared, visible, ultraviolet, X-rays, gamma rays", "X-rays, gamma rays, ultraviolet, visible, infrared, radio waves, microwaves", "Gamma rays, X-rays, visible, ultraviolet, infrared, microwaves, radio waves", "Gamma rays, X-rays, ultraviolet, visible, infrared, microwaves, radio waves"]', 3, 'Gamma rays have the shortest wavelength (highest frequency and photon energy) and radio waves the longest. Ultraviolet lies just shorter than visible light, infrared just longer.', '{"0": "This is the order of increasing frequency, i.e. decreasing wavelength.", "1": "Gamma rays are shorter than X-rays, and microwaves are shorter than radio waves.", "2": "Ultraviolet has a shorter wavelength than visible light."}'),
  ('PHY-7281', 'Electronics', 'Rectification', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 13 (Electronics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 10', 'A 50 Hz AC supply is fed to a full-wave bridge rectifier. The frequency of the pulses in the output is', '["100 Hz", "50 Hz", "25 Hz", "200 Hz"]', 0, 'A full-wave rectifier turns both halves of each cycle into a pulse in the same direction, giving two pulses per input cycle: 100 Hz. A half-wave rectifier gives 50 Hz.', '{"1": "50 Hz is the output pulse rate of a half-wave rectifier.", "2": "25 Hz would mean one pulse every two cycles.", "3": "200 Hz would need four pulses per cycle."}'),
  ('PHY-7282', 'Electronics', 'Rectification', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 13 (Electronics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 10', 'In a bridge rectifier, how many of the four diodes conduct at any instant while current flows?', '["1", "2", "3", "4"]', 1, 'In each half cycle, one pair of opposite diodes is forward biased and carries the current through the load in the same direction; the other pair is reverse biased.', '{"0": "One diode would leave the current with no return path.", "2": "Three diodes cannot be forward biased together in a bridge.", "3": "If all four conducted, the supply would be short-circuited."}'),
  ('PHY-7283', 'Electronics', 'Rectification', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 13 (Electronics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 10', 'A bridge rectifier uses silicon diodes, each with a forward voltage drop of 0.7 V. If the peak input voltage is 20 V, the peak output voltage across the load is about', '["19.3 V", "20 V", "18.6 V", "17.9 V"]', 2, 'Current passes through two diodes in series in each half cycle, so the loss is 2 × 0.7 = 1.4 V: 20 − 1.4 = 18.6 V.', '{"0": "19.3 V allows for only one diode drop (as in a half-wave rectifier).", "1": "20 V ignores the diode drops.", "3": "17.9 V subtracts three diode drops."}'),
  ('PHY-7284', 'Electronics', 'PN junction', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 13 (Electronics)', 'When a PN junction is reverse biased,', '["the depletion region narrows and a large current flows", "the depletion region disappears", "majority carriers flow easily across the junction", "the depletion region widens and the potential barrier increases"]', 3, 'Reverse bias pulls holes and electrons away from the junction, widening the depletion region and raising the barrier. Only a tiny leakage current of minority carriers flows.', '{"0": "Narrowing and large current describe forward bias.", "1": "The depletion region grows under reverse bias; it does not vanish.", "2": "Majority carriers are blocked by the higher barrier."}'),
  ('PHY-7285', 'Electronics', 'PN junction', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 13 (Electronics)', 'A silicon diode (forward voltage 0.7 V) is connected in series with a 1.0 kΩ resistor across a 5.0 V DC supply, forward biased. The current is', '["4.3 mA", "5.0 mA", "5.7 mA", "0"]', 0, 'The diode takes about 0.7 V when conducting, leaving 4.3 V across the resistor: I = 4.3/1000 = 4.3 mA.', '{"1": "5.0 mA ignores the diode''s forward voltage.", "2": "5.7 mA adds the diode voltage instead of subtracting it.", "3": "A forward-biased diode conducts once the voltage exceeds about 0.7 V."}'),
  ('PHY-7286', 'Electronics', 'Rectification', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 13 (Electronics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 10', 'A lamp is connected to an AC supply through a single diode in series. Compared with the lamp connected directly (and ignoring the diode''s voltage drop and any change in the lamp''s resistance), its average power is', '["a quarter", "half", "the same", "zero"]', 1, 'The diode passes only one half of each cycle (half-wave rectification). During that half the lamp gets the same power as before; during the other half it gets none. Average power halves.', '{"0": "A quarter would mean the voltage itself had been halved.", "2": "Half of every cycle is blocked, so the power falls.", "3": "The diode conducts on alternate half cycles, so current does flow."}'),
  ('PHY-7287', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Photon energy', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 14–16 (Modern and nuclear physics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 11–13', 'The energy of a photon of wavelength 620 nm is about (hc = 1240 eV nm)', '["1.2 eV", "3.1 eV", "2.0 eV", "0.5 eV"]', 2, 'E = hc/λ = 1240 eV nm/620 nm = 2.0 eV (= 3.2 × 10⁻¹⁹ J).', '{"0": "1.2 eV uses λ ≈ 1000 nm.", "1": "3.1 eV is for 400 nm violet light.", "3": "0.5 eV inverts the ratio (λ/hc)."}'),
  ('PHY-7288', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Photons', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 14–16 (Modern and nuclear physics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 11–13', 'A 2.0 mW laser emits light of wavelength 660 nm. About how many photons does it emit each second? (h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, c = 3.0 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹)', '["6.6 × 10¹⁸", "3.0 × 10⁻¹⁹", "3.3 × 10¹⁵", "6.6 × 10¹⁵"]', 3, 'Photon energy hc/λ = (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.0 × 10⁸)/(660 × 10⁻⁹) ≈ 3.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J. Number per second = 2.0 × 10⁻³/3.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ ≈ 6.6 × 10¹⁵.', '{"0": "6.6 × 10¹⁸ uses 2.0 W instead of 2.0 mW.", "1": "3.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ is the energy of one photon in joules.", "2": "3.3 × 10¹⁵ uses 1.0 mW."}'),
  ('PHY-7289', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Photoelectric effect', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 14–16 (Modern and nuclear physics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 11–13', 'Light of wavelength 400 nm falls on a metal with work function 2.0 eV. The maximum kinetic energy of the emitted electrons is (hc = 1240 eV nm)', '["1.1 eV", "3.1 eV", "5.1 eV", "0.55 eV"]', 0, 'Photon energy = 1240/400 = 3.1 eV. K_max = hf − φ = 3.1 − 2.0 = 1.1 eV.', '{"1": "3.1 eV is the photon energy, ignoring the work function.", "2": "5.1 eV adds the work function instead of subtracting it.", "3": "0.55 eV halves the answer."}'),
  ('PHY-7290', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Photoelectric effect', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 14–16 (Modern and nuclear physics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 11–13', 'In a photoelectric experiment, the intensity of the light is doubled while its frequency (above threshold) is unchanged. Then', '["the maximum kinetic energy of the photoelectrons doubles", "the number of photoelectrons per second doubles, but their maximum kinetic energy is unchanged", "both the number and the maximum kinetic energy double", "the threshold frequency halves"]', 1, 'Doubling intensity doubles the number of photons per second, so twice as many electrons are emitted. Each photon still has energy hf, so K_max = hf − φ is unchanged.', '{"0": "K_max depends only on frequency, not on intensity.", "2": "Only the number of electrons changes, not their maximum energy.", "3": "The threshold frequency is a property of the metal (φ/h)."}'),
  ('PHY-7291', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Atomic spectra', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 14–16 (Modern and nuclear physics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 11–13', 'In hydrogen, Eₙ = −13.6/n² eV. The wavelength of the light emitted when an electron falls from n = 3 to n = 2 is about (hc = 1240 eV nm)', '["486 nm", "122 nm", "656 nm", "1875 nm"]', 2, 'ΔE = 13.6(1/4 − 1/9) = 1.89 eV. λ = 1240/1.89 ≈ 656 nm, the red Balmer line (Hα).', '{"0": "486 nm is the 4 → 2 line.", "1": "122 nm is the 2 → 1 Lyman line.", "3": "1875 nm is the 4 → 3 Paschen line."}'),
  ('PHY-7292', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Atomic spectra', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 14–16 (Modern and nuclear physics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 11–13', 'A sample of hydrogen atoms is excited to the n = 4 level. As they return to the ground state, the greatest number of different spectral lines that can be emitted is', '["3", "4", "10", "6"]', 3, 'Any pair of levels among n = 1, 2, 3, 4 can give a line: 4 → 3, 4 → 2, 4 → 1, 3 → 2, 3 → 1, 2 → 1. That is n(n − 1)/2 = 6.', '{"0": "3 counts only the jumps straight down one level at a time.", "1": "4 counts the levels, not the transitions between them.", "2": "10 is the count for atoms excited to n = 5."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, options, answer, why, wrong, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, 'Physics', s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PHY-7293', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Atomic spectra', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 14–16 (Modern and nuclear physics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 11–13', 'With Eₙ = −13.6/n² eV, the shortest wavelength in the Balmer series of hydrogen is about (hc = 1240 eV nm)', '["365 nm", "656 nm", "91 nm", "122 nm"]', 0, 'The Balmer series ends on n = 2. The shortest wavelength comes from n = ∞ → 2: ΔE = 13.6/4 = 3.4 eV, λ = 1240/3.4 ≈ 365 nm.', '{"1": "656 nm is the longest Balmer line (3 → 2).", "2": "91 nm is the shortest Lyman line (∞ → 1).", "3": "122 nm is the longest Lyman line (2 → 1)."}'),
  ('PHY-7294', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Atomic spectra', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 14–16 (Modern and nuclear physics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 11–13', 'A hydrogen atom is in its n = 3 state. The energy needed to ionize it from this state is (Eₙ = −13.6/n² eV)', '["12.09 eV", "1.51 eV", "13.6 eV", "4.53 eV"]', 1, 'E₃ = −13.6/9 = −1.51 eV. Ionization means raising the electron to E = 0, which needs 1.51 eV.', '{"0": "12.09 eV is the energy to excite the atom from n = 1 to n = 3.", "2": "13.6 eV is the ionization energy from the ground state.", "3": "4.53 eV divides 13.6 by 3 instead of 9."}'),
  ('PHY-7295', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Half-life', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 14–16 (Modern and nuclear physics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 11–13', 'After 24 days, one-eighth of a radioactive sample remains undecayed. Its half-life is', '["3 days", "12 days", "8 days", "6 days"]', 2, '1/8 = (1/2)³, so 24 days is three half-lives: T½ = 8 days.', '{"0": "3 days divides 24 by 8, treating the fraction as a number of half-lives.", "1": "12 days is two half-lives giving 1/4.", "3": "6 days would give (1/2)⁴ = 1/16."}'),
  ('PHY-7296', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Half-life and decay constant', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 14–16 (Modern and nuclear physics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 11–13', 'A radioactive sample contains 2.0 × 10¹⁸ nuclei and its half-life is 6.93 h. Its activity is about', '["2.0 × 10¹⁷ Bq", "1.4 × 10¹³ Bq", "2.8 × 10⁻⁵ Bq", "5.6 × 10¹³ Bq"]', 3, 'λ = 0.693/T½ = 0.693/(6.93 × 3600 s) ≈ 2.78 × 10⁻⁵ s⁻¹. A = λN = 2.78 × 10⁻⁵ × 2.0 × 10¹⁸ ≈ 5.6 × 10¹³ Bq.', '{"0": "2.0 × 10¹⁷ Bq uses λ = 0.1 h⁻¹ as if it were per second.", "1": "1.4 × 10¹³ Bq uses a quarter of the correct λ.", "2": "2.8 × 10⁻⁵ is the decay constant in s⁻¹, not the activity."}'),
  ('PHY-7297', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Half-life', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 14–16 (Modern and nuclear physics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 11–13', 'What fraction of the original nuclei in a radioactive sample decays during the third half-life?', '["1/8", "1/2", "7/8", "1/4"]', 0, 'After two half-lives 1/4 remains; after three, 1/8 remains. So during the third half-life 1/4 − 1/8 = 1/8 of the original nuclei decay.', '{"1": "1/2 of the nuclei present at the start of that period decay, but that is only 1/8 of the original.", "2": "7/8 is the total that has decayed after three half-lives.", "3": "1/4 is what decays during the second half-life."}'),
  ('PHY-7298', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Half-life', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 14–16 (Modern and nuclear physics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 11–13', 'Two radioactive samples A and B start with equal numbers of nuclei. A has a half-life of 2 h and B of 4 h. After 8 h, the ratio of undecayed nuclei N_A : N_B is', '["1 : 2", "1 : 4", "4 : 1", "1 : 16"]', 1, 'In 8 h, A passes through 4 half-lives (1/16 left) and B through 2 (1/4 left). Ratio (1/16) : (1/4) = 1 : 4.', '{"0": "1 : 2 compares the half-lives, not the amounts left.", "2": "4 : 1 inverts the ratio; the shorter-lived A has fewer nuclei left.", "3": "1 : 16 is the fraction of A left, not the ratio to B."}'),
  ('PHY-7299', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Radioactive decay', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 14–16 (Modern and nuclear physics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 11–13', 'A nucleus ²³⁸₉₂U emits one alpha particle and then two beta-minus particles. The final nucleus has', '["mass number 234 and atomic number 90", "mass number 234 and atomic number 94", "mass number 234 and atomic number 92", "mass number 230 and atomic number 90"]', 2, 'Alpha emission: A − 4, Z − 2 → ²³⁴₉₀. Each β⁻: A unchanged, Z + 1 → ²³⁴₉₂. The result is an isotope of the original element (uranium).', '{"0": "Z = 90 is the nucleus after the alpha decay alone, before the two beta decays.", "1": "Z = 94 counts the beta decays but forgets the alpha particle''s charge.", "3": "Mass number 230 removes 8 units, as if two alphas were emitted."}'),
  ('PHY-7300', 'Modern and nuclear physics', 'Radioactive decay', 'hard', 'Difficult set; PMDC MDCAT 2025 Physics unit 14–16 (Modern and nuclear physics); NUMS MDCAT 2026 Physics unit 11–13', 'Radium-226 (Z = 88) decays through a series of steps to lead-206 (Z = 82). The numbers of alpha and beta-minus particles emitted are', '["5 alpha and 6 beta", "4 alpha and 5 beta", "10 alpha and 2 beta", "5 alpha and 4 beta"]', 3, 'Only alpha decay changes A: (226 − 206)/4 = 5 alphas, which lower Z by 10 to 78. To reach 82, four β⁻ decays must raise Z by 4.', '{"0": "6 beta decays would end at Z = 84 (polonium).", "1": "4 alpha decays would change the mass number by only 16.", "2": "10 alphas would remove 40 mass units."}')
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

-- 9 questions are not on the PMDC MDCAT syllabus (kept out of the MDCAT bank and mocks).
